WEBVTT

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:03.575
This is what I wrote down.
这就是我写下来的。

00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:16.355
I said the scene in the bar with Ted describing his future wedding to the woman he just met, followed by the look on his face when he sees Robin for the first time.
我说的是酒吧里泰德对刚认识的女人描述他未来的婚礼的那场戏，紧接着是他第一次看到罗宾时的表情。

00:00:16.355 --> 00:00:20.407
This is 100% when every woman fell in love with Ted.
这绝对是所有女性爱上泰德的时刻。

00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:20.907
Yes!
没错！

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:21.998
I'm like this?
我就是这样的吗？

00:00:21.998 --> 00:00:25.738
Oh my god, where was this guy when I was dating?
天哪，我约会的时候这家伙在哪儿？

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:44.939
New York City, when I see you, please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
纽约市，当我看到你时，请允许我在纽约市把一切都告诉你。

00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:48.899
Well, hello, welcome to How We Made Your Mother.
嗯，大家好，欢迎来到《我们是如何创造你母亲的》。

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:49.899
I'm Josh Radder.
我是乔什·拉德。

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:52.219
I am joined as ever by my friend Craig Thomas.
和往常一样，我的朋友克雷格·托马斯也加入了我们。

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:53.219
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.219
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:58.700
Uh, Craig and I worked on a show together for many years.
呃，我和克雷格一起合作过一个节目很多年。

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.219
It was called How I Met Your Mother.
它叫《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:07.060
I played Ted Mosvio on that show, Craig co-created the show with Carter Bayes, and now we're going through episode by episode.
我在那个节目里扮演泰德·莫斯维奥，克雷格和卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧，现在我们将逐集回顾。

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.099
We're talking about it all.
我们无所不谈。

00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:17.060
We have some episodes that are just episodes, and some episodes are special episodes, and some episodes are extra, extra special, special episodes.
我们有些集就是普通的集，有些集是特别集，还有些集是额外、额外特别的特别集。

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.019
And this is one of the extra, extra, extra, extra, extra, special episodes.
而这就是那些额外、额外、额外、额外、额外的特别集之一。

00:01:21.019 --> 00:01:27.120
Because we are joined by the office ladies, Jenna Fisher and Angela Kinsey, hi.
因为我们迎来了《办公室女孩》的主播珍娜·费舍尔和安吉拉·金斯利，嗨。

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.719
Jenna, Angela, hi guys.
珍娜，安吉拉，嗨，各位。

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:31.310
lovely introduction.
精彩的介绍。

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.503
I just wing it.
我只是临场发挥。

00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:33.697
I just wing it.
我只是随性发挥。

00:01:33.697 --> 00:01:39.662
You guys are you've already shown us you're much more prepared than we are.
你们俩已经向我们展示了你们比我们准备得充分得多。

00:01:39.662 --> 00:01:40.935
We're delighted.
我们很高兴。

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:43.560
We're delighted to have you here.
很高兴你们能来。

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.489
We're delighted as I assume people know by now.
我想大家现在都知道了，我们非常高兴。

00:01:46.489 --> 00:01:51.038
We are being produced by the Office Ladies Network for our second season.
我们的第二季是由办公室女孩网络制作的。

00:01:51.038 --> 00:01:54.840
We were just so thrilled to get the invitation from you guys.
我们非常高兴能收到你们的邀请。

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.719
And yeah, we're just going to talk about lots of things.
是的，我们今天要聊很多事情。

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:01.079
We're going to talk about the pilot of how we made a mother.
我们要聊聊《我们是如何创造一个母亲的》的试播集。

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:02.519
We're going to talk about the office.
我们要聊聊《办公室》。

00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:06.319
We're going to talk about making TV, being on TV shows for a long time.
我们要聊聊制作电视剧、长期参演电视剧。

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:12.039
People thinking you're the character when you're not, you're a human being, all kinds of stuff.
人们把当成你实际上并不是的角色，你只是个真人，各种各样的事情。

00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:14.080
So yeah, well, thanks for joining us.
所以，谢谢你们加入我们。

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.479
Well, this is Trezys, right?
嗯，这算礼尚往来，对吧？

00:02:15.479 --> 00:02:17.000
We did your show and you're doing our show.
我们上了你们的节目，而你们在上我们的节目。

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.479
So thank you for being great.
所以谢谢你们这么棒。

00:02:18.479 --> 00:02:22.759
We got to dig in the customer survey and then also talk about how you guys made your show.
我们得深入探讨客户调查，还要聊聊你们是怎么制作你们的节目的。

00:02:22.759 --> 00:02:24.479
And we're so tickled to be here today.
我们今天能来到这里感到非常高兴。

00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:27.000
Well, thanks, thanks so much for joining us.
嗯，谢谢，非常感谢你们加入我们。

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:39.280
So I guess, I mean, we talked a little bit about this, but you know, I always felt a special kinship with the office because as Craig says, it felt like we were in the same high school class together.
所以我想，我的意思是，我们之前稍微聊过这个，但我一直对《办公室》有着一种特殊的亲切感，因为正如克雷格所说，感觉我们就像在同一个高中班级里一样。

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.639
Like we started, I think, did you guys start a year before us?
就像我们开始的时候，我想，你们比我们早一年开始吗？

00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:43.479
I think you might have.
我想你们可能是的。

00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:44.319
Yes, one year.
是的，早一年。

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.000
You started a year before us, 2004.
你们比我们早一年开始，2004年。

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.560
And did you guys go nine or 10 years?
你们拍了九年还是十年？

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.439
Nine.
九年。

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:49.280
Nine years.
九年。

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.000
So we all did nine seasons.
所以我们都拍了九季。

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.000
We all hit the big network old chunky seasons.
我们都经历过传统电视网的长季数时代。

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.240
Yeah, it's not like that anymore.
对，现在情况完全不同了。

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.680
We weathered a couple of union strikes together.
我们一起经历了几次工会罢工。

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.639
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:04.599
Yeah.
对。

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:06.840
But it all, I always felt a special kinship with you guys.
但总的来说，我一直对你们有一种特殊的亲切感。

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.840
Just we were on the air at the same time.
就因为我们在同一时期播出。

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.159
You know, for the three of us, this was a big thing for us.
你知道，对于我们三个人来说，这是一件大事。

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:17.360
It wasn't like we were coming off another big hit show or something.
这不像是我们刚拍完另一部大热剧或者别的什么。

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:31.360
This really introduced us to audiences, you know, beyond what we were doing in the theater or improv as Angela was doing still at night when she was doing the office, which I was so charmed to hear that you were still doing improv at night.
这真正让我们走入了观众的视野，你知道的，超越了我们在剧场或即兴表演的范畴，就像安吉拉在拍《办公室》时晚上还在做即兴表演一样，当我听到你晚上还在做即兴表演时，我感到非常迷人。

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:42.599
But it really felt like we were in the same kind of class and we talked about these shows, nine year shows, 200 episodes, 22, 24 season, they're just not being made anymore.
但真的感觉我们就像是同一届的，我们谈论这些剧，九年的剧，200集，每季22、24集，这样的剧现在已经不再制作了。

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:48.120
So it feels like we didn't know we were at the kind of tail end of a way to make television.
所以感觉我们当时并不知道自己正处于制作电视节目的尾声。

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.159
But it was, you know, it was really exciting.
但你知道，那真的很激动人心。

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:53.060
What are you guys feeling these days about all that?
你们现在对这一切有什么感觉？

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.099
Well, you know, it's all I ever wanted to do.
嗯，你知道的，这是我一直想做的事。

00:03:55.099 --> 00:04:04.139
It's why I drove my Mazda 323 hatchback with my cat, Andi, across the country from Missouri to California.
这就是为什么我带着我的猫安迪，开着马自达323两厢车，从密苏里州一路横跨全国来到加利福尼亚。

00:04:04.139 --> 00:04:06.860
I wanted to be on Cheers.
我想参演《欢乐酒店》。

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.500
Cheers was the show I watched with my family.
《欢乐酒店》是我和家人一起看的节目。

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:16.579
Cheers was the show that like, it didn't matter that I was a teenager and I didn't want to be in the same room with my parents most of the time.
《欢乐酒店》这个节目就是这样，哪怕我当时是个青少年，大部分时间不想和父母待在同一个房间里。

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:19.220
I did want to be in the same room with them to watch Cheers.
但我确实想和他们待在同一个房间里看《欢乐酒店》。

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.459
And I wanted to be Diane Chambers, that's what I wanted.
我想成为黛安·钱伯斯，这就是我想要的。

00:04:23.459 --> 00:04:26.300
I just was like, that is such a great role.
我当时就想，那真是一个极好的角色。

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.379
Gosh, could I ever be on an ensemble comedy like that?
天哪，我能有机会参演那样的一部群戏喜剧吗？

00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:47.620
So the fact that I got to do it is just mind blowing and I'm so grateful and I'm glad that I got to do it before things changed and before now it's, you know, I'm glad I got to be part of that, I guess, I don't know old version of television.
所以能有机会参演，我真的感到不可思议，我非常感激，也很高兴能在事情改变之前、在现在这个……你知道的，我很高兴我能成为那种旧版电视时代的一部分，我想，我不知道。

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.060
We didn't know we were at the end.
我们当时并不知道自己正处于终点。

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:49.899
We didn't know.
我们当时不知道。

00:04:49.899 --> 00:04:54.860
We didn't know and we are two of the final shows that can say we were in that same category as Cheers.
我们当时并不知道，而且我们是最后几部能够自豪地说我们和《欢乐酒店》属于同一类别的剧集之一。

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.420
Cheers was my all-time or two, Jenna.
《欢乐酒店》一直是我最喜欢的剧集之一，詹娜。

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.100
That was like why I wanted to be a writer.
那简直是我想要成为一名编剧的原因。

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.420
That is my go-to show and we got to do that.
那是我的首选剧集，而我们居然有机会参与其中。

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.540
We actually got to do that, didn't we?
我们真的有机会做到这一点，对吧？

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:13.019
We talked about that NBC Thursday night lineup of Cosby Show, Family Ties, Cheers Night Court LA Law.
我们谈论过NBC周四晚间的黄金档阵容，包括《考斯比一家》、《亲情纽带》、《欢乐酒店》、《夜间法庭》和《洛城法律》。

00:05:13.019 --> 00:05:18.459
Like to me, that was like the three hour block of television to end all three hour blocks of television.
对我来说，那简直是空前绝后的三小时电视节目黄金档。

00:05:18.459 --> 00:05:36.540
I also think it's astonishing, and Angela, I wanna hear what brought you to California and how you ended up on the show, but there's something incredible when I think about like a 16-year-old boy decided what I was gonna do with my life and like how brilliant like that he knew, you know?
我也觉得这很令人惊叹，安吉拉，我想听听是什么把你带到了加州，你是怎么最终加入这部剧的，但当我想到一个16岁的男孩决定了我这一生要做什么，以及他是多么聪慧地预见到了这一切时，真的是不可思议，你知道吗？

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.660
And I think the reason what I'm doing now is different than what was his name?
而且我觉得，我现在做的事和那个人的名字叫什么……

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.500
It's me.
是我。

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.740
Am I not following you?
我是没跟上你的思路吗？

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.300
Okay.
好吧。

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:50.798
But you know, just to have the vision, I think I watched, you know, I loved Cheers, but I also loved Alex B.
但你知道，仅仅是有那份远见，我想我当时看……你知道，我很喜欢《欢乐酒店》，但我也很喜欢亚历克斯·B。

00:05:50.798 --> 00:05:51.298
Keaton.
基顿。

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.300
I loved family ties.
我喜欢《亲情纽带》。

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.819
It also took place in my hometown in Columbus, Ohio.
它甚至就发生我的家乡俄亥俄州哥伦布市。

00:05:54.819 --> 00:06:03.420
And just to, I think there's something incredible when you have very strong vision and you're able to kind of say, it's like Babe Ruth pointing to where the home runs going.
而且我觉得，当你拥有非常强烈的愿景，并且能够某种程度上说出那样的话时，这真的很不可思议，就像贝比·鲁斯指着本垒打将要飞去的方向一样。

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.819
Like it doesn't happen all the time in our lives, right?
这在我们的生活中并不总是会发生，对吧？

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:09.819
I don't know that we can magically manifest everything we want.
我不知道我们是否能凭空变出我们想要的一切。

00:06:09.819 --> 00:06:12.540
Obviously there's disappointment, there's setback, there's failure.
显然会有失望、会有挫折、会有失败。

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:19.060
But it's incredible that you loved something so much and then you're like, I'm getting in the car, I'm gonna go do it.
但令人难以置信的是，你那么热爱某样东西，然后你就想：我要上车，我要去实现它。

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.259
And you did, you absolutely did it.
而你确实做到了，你绝对做到了。

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:26.779
Like, Pam is the Diane of the office.
就像，帕姆就是《办公室》里的黛安。

00:06:26.779 --> 00:06:29.699
You know, like it really, you did it, congrats.
你知道的，真的，你做到了，恭喜。

00:06:29.699 --> 00:06:34.500
I did it, I did it, and you know, and it was with many setbacks along the way.
我做到了，我真的做到了，而且你知道，这一路上遇到了很多挫折。

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:49.660
I mean, like you said, Josh, for the three of us, I don't know how long you were knocking around, but Angela and I, we were both knocking around for like eight, 10 years before we landed on the office, you know, just unknown struggling actors trying to make our way.
我的意思是，就像你说的，乔什，对于我们三个来说，我不知道你混了多久，但安吉拉和我，我们在加入《办公室》之前都摸爬滚打 了大约八到十年，你知道的，就是默默无闻、苦苦挣扎、寻找出路的演员。

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.779
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:06:50.779 --> 00:06:54.180
Angela, where are you from, by the way?
安吉拉，顺便问一下，你是哪里人？

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.740
Oh gosh, that's a different podcast.
哦天哪，那是另一个播客的内容了。

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:02.220
I mean, I was born in Louisiana, families from Texas, Louisiana.
我是说，我出生在路易斯安那州，家里人来自德克萨斯州和路易斯安那州。

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.060
When I was two years old, I moved to Jakarta, Indonesia.
我两岁的时候，搬到了印度尼西亚雅加达。

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.699
I lived in Indonesia until I was 14 and then came back stateside.
我在印尼一直住到14岁，然后回到了美国本土。

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:13.899
But I will tell you, growing up overseas, I didn't have television.
但我得告诉你，在海外长大的时候，我没有电视看。

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:20.259
And when we would come to the States in the summer, I would be glued.
每当夏天我们回到美国，我就会目不转睛地盯着看。

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:23.699
You could not prime me away from the TV.
你根本别想把我从电视机前拉开。

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:30.300
We didn't get an English speaking show in Indonesia until I was older and it was Little House on the Prairie.
直到我大一点，印尼才开始播放英语节目，那是《草原小屋》。

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.980
So when I came to the- That's where you hold your comedy chops?
所以当我来到——你的喜剧造诣就是从那里培养出来的？

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:36.060
Like if only they can be as funny as this show.
就像只要他们能像这个节目一样搞笑就行。

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.379
Yeah, exactly.
对，没错。

00:07:37.379 --> 00:07:39.699
No, it was always so dramatic.
不，它总是那么戏剧化。

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:41.100
It's like the baby's got a fever.
就像孩子发烧了。

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:41.939
It's Christmas Eve.
正好是平安夜。

00:07:41.939 --> 00:07:43.699
Oh, go to town.
哦，天哪。

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:52.060
And, you know, it was always like, but when I'd come to the States in the summer, I was like, I was watching all of these shows that were just amazing.
而且，你知道，总是这样，但每当夏天我回到美国，我就像在看所有这些让人惊叹的节目。

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.220
And that's all I wanted to do.
那是我唯一想做的事。

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.339
I was like, that thing, that's what I want.
我就想，那个东西，那就是我想要的。

00:07:56.339 --> 00:07:59.180
Was there a specific one or two that grabbed you that you were like, I love it?
有没有哪一两个特别吸引你，让你觉得“我太喜欢了”？

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:00.579
Oh my goodness, I loved all of them.
哦天哪，我都喜欢。

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:01.860
I loved family ties too.
我也很喜欢《家族风暴》 (Family Ties)。

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:06.060
I loved Mallory, but Mallory had such a distinct point of view.
我非常喜欢 Mallory，但 Mallory 有着如此鲜明的观点。

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:16.180
I always say, the only reason I know scuba stands for self-contained underwater breathing apparatus this because it was in an episode of show and learn it for the rest of the game.
我常说，我唯一知道 scuba 是 self-contained underwater breathing apparatus（自携式水下呼吸器）的原因，是因为它出现在一集《Show and Learn》里，并在游戏的其余时间里记住了它。

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:16.687
That's right.
没错。

00:08:16.687 --> 00:08:17.779
You know the rhythm of that.
你懂那个节奏。

00:08:17.779 --> 00:08:18.279
Yeah.
是的。

00:08:18.279 --> 00:08:19.420
Same in Cheers too.
《欢乐酒店》里也是一样。

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:27.740
I feel like I know like things, random things from Cheers, like Albania, Albania, it borders on the Adriatic.
我觉得我知道一些事情，比如从《欢乐酒店》里学到的随机事情，像阿尔巴尼亚，阿尔巴尼亚，它毗邻亚得里亚海。

00:08:28.579 --> 00:08:31.459
They were helping coach, you know, on his test.
他们当时在帮 Coach 辅导考试，你知道的。

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.220
I mean, anyway, those, those were shows that meant so much to me.
我的意思是，不管怎样，那些节目对我意义重大。

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.179
And all I wanted to do was be on an ensemble TV comedy.
我当时只想参演一部群戏电视喜剧。

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:42.100
And I did this really dorky thing.
我做了一件非常傻气的事。

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.940
And it's one of the reasons why Jen and I are best friends because we are these people.
这也是珍和我成为最好朋友的原因之一，因为我们就是这种人。

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:46.860
We're me and a gal.
我和一个女孩。

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.139
We were working at a restaurant on Sunset Boulevard.
我们当时在日落大道的一家餐厅工作。

00:08:50.139 --> 00:08:55.259
And we had heard that Jim Carrey like would sort of say his things that he wanted and manifest them.
我们听说金·凯瑞会说出他想要的东西并将其具象化。

00:08:55.259 --> 00:09:00.539
And we drove up to Mulholland and we yelled out into like the Hollywood skyline.
我们开车上了穆赫兰大道，朝着好莱坞的天际线大喊。

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:03.139
And I yelled this, Hollywood, do you hear me?
我喊道：好莱坞，你听到我说话了吗？

00:09:03.139 --> 00:09:08.139
I wanna be on a Thursday night television comedy ensemble.
我想上周四晚上的电视喜剧群戏。

00:09:08.139 --> 00:09:12.742
It preferably in the time in the friend's time slot and you did it.
最好是在《老友记》的那个时间段，而且你做到了。

00:09:12.742 --> 00:09:39.259
This is unbelievable There's like a little footnote in your prayer Yeah, but I did I yelled at I wrote it out really specific because my mom used to always say God is in the details Right, so I wrote out really specific you know I think I told Craig this but I had my My same like kind of working prayer as you guys had I said I want to be on a hit show I wanted to be an ensemble show.
这简直难以置信，这就像是你祈祷中的一个小脚注。是的，但我确实这么做了，我大喊大叫，我写得很具体，因为我妈妈过去常说魔鬼藏在细节里，对吧，所以我写得很具体，你知道，我想我告诉过克雷格，但我有和你们一样的日常祈祷。我说我想上一部热门节目，我想演一部群戏节目。

00:09:39.259 --> 00:09:43.940
I wanted to be young writers who've never had a hit show before because I thought it was like lightning in a bottle.
我希望是一群从未有过热门节目的年轻编剧，因为我觉得这就像千载难逢的奇迹。

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.860
I just wanted to be with like generationally, like I wanted to be the same age.
我只想和同代人在一起，就像我想和大家年龄相仿一样。

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.419
And I said, I want to be the lead of it.
我说，我想成为主角。

00:09:51.419 --> 00:09:52.980
And I want it to be in prime time.
而且我要它在黄金时段播出。

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:53.980
Yes.
是的。

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:54.980
Wow.
哇。

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:55.980
Ticked all the boxes.
全都说中了。

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:56.980
No, it was it.
不，就是这样。

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:02.580
But I think it's a good thing for listeners who either have artistic aspirations or any other.
但我觉得这对有艺术追求或其它追求的听众来说是一件好事。

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:17.240
I think any founder of a company has to have that same kind of insane vision slash working prayer where if you think about anything man made like a chair like it was in your imagination before like you see it and then you make it.
我认为任何公司的创始人都必须具备那种疯狂的远见加上某种执着的祈愿：如果你想想任何人工制造的东西，比如一把椅子，它在你想象中先存在，然后你看到它，接着你把它制造出来。

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:36.457
So I really do I do believe that having some sort of vision for where you want to go is the best way to get there you know because otherwise you're just kind of popping around on the high seas of you have to be delusional a little bit right you have to be a little healthy amount of delusion.
所以我真的相信，对你想去的地方有一种愿景是到达那里的最佳方式，你知道的，因为否则你就会像在公海上到处乱撞，你必须有点妄想症，对吧，你必须有适度的健康妄想。

00:10:36.457 --> 00:10:41.459
There was no reason Carter and I should have been running a TV show in 2005.
2005年，我和卡特（Carter）没有任何理由去运营一个电视节目。

00:10:41.459 --> 00:10:45.013
We maybe shouldn't have even been staffed on a sitcom.
我们甚至可能都不应该在情景喜剧里当职员。

00:10:45.013 --> 00:10:50.673
We had never written on a sitcom when we created and started running How Much Another.
当我们创作并开始运营《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，我们从未写过情景喜剧。

00:10:50.673 --> 00:10:54.754
We had never written on a multi-camera proper sitcom that way.
我们从来没有以那种方式为多机位正统情景喜剧写过剧本。

00:10:54.754 --> 00:11:04.297
You need that weird thing of like, I know I have to do this and I will just pursue this just in an absolutely monomaniacal, illogical way, right?
你需要那种奇怪的感觉：我知道我必须这么做，而且我会以绝对专断、不合逻辑的方式去追求它，对吧？

00:11:04.297 --> 00:11:07.720
You kind of need a little bit of crazy to get there.
你确实需要一点疯狂才能达到目标。

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:13.440
And even, you know, I'm just getting this image of Angela shouting at the, it was it by the Hollywood sign?
甚至，你知道的，我脑海中浮现出安吉拉对著……那是好莱坞标志附近吗？

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.000
Well, Holland, no, no, we drove up to Mulholland, but preferably.
嗯，穆荷兰，不，不，我们开车去了穆荷兰，不过最好是。

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:23.826
I shouted, I'm sorry, I didn't shout to the valley side of Mulholland to the Hollywood Sure.
我喊了，对不起，我不是对著穆荷兰的山谷那边喊的，我是对著好莱坞……

00:11:23.826 --> 00:11:24.326
Yeah.
是啊。

00:11:24.326 --> 00:11:25.736
No, that was smart.
不，那很明智。

00:11:25.736 --> 00:11:29.001
Your life could have taken a different turn.
你的人生可能会走向不同的方向。

00:11:29.001 --> 00:11:30.188
Yeah, it worked.
是的，管用了。

00:11:30.059 --> 00:11:30.559
It worked.
管用了。

00:11:30.559 --> 00:11:31.059
Yeah.
对。

00:11:31.059 --> 00:11:32.346
Who knows what would happen?
谁知道会发生什么呢？

00:11:32.346 --> 00:11:34.690
Although my show did end up shooting in the valley.
虽然我的节目最后确实是在山谷拍摄的。

00:11:34.379 --> 00:11:39.944
So, you know, but it's also it's a very Ted Mosby thing to kind of shouted the heavens, make it rain.
所以，你知道的，但这也很像泰德·莫斯比（Ted Mosby）会对苍天大喊“让它下雨”那种风格。

00:11:39.944 --> 00:11:42.974
I mean, he had his moments of like deep intense prayer.
我的意思是，他也有过那种深沉而强烈的祈愿时刻。

00:11:42.974 --> 00:11:47.602
I also I'm struck by the fact that you guys both said I want to be on ensemble show.
我也对一件事印象深刻，你们俩都说过“我想出演群戏节目”。

00:11:47.602 --> 00:11:49.806
I want to be a part of a great ensemble.
“我想成为伟大群戏的一部分。”

00:11:49.806 --> 00:11:53.938
And I think that that, you know, any great show is not one great character.
我认为，你知道的，任何伟大的节目都不是某一个伟大的角色。

00:11:53.938 --> 00:11:55.426
It's many great characters.
而是许多伟大的角色。

00:11:55.426 --> 00:12:00.825
It's anywhere you turn, you're delighted, whatever configuration of the characters are in a scene.
无论你转向哪里，无论这些角色在场景中如何组合，你都会感到高兴。

00:12:00.825 --> 00:12:02.202
You're delighted by that.
你对此感到高兴。

00:12:02.202 --> 00:12:08.483
For me, that was like my theater background, you know, just being in companies of every summer and in grad school.
对我来说，这就像我的戏剧背景，你知道的，每年夏天和在研究生院都加入剧团。

00:12:08.483 --> 00:12:12.340
I loved being in the trenches with other actors and putting on a show.
我喜欢和其他演员一起在战壕里奋斗并上演一出好戏。

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:22.990
And I wrote you this little ditty to send you in New York City.
我给你写了这首小曲，送你去纽约市。

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:35.690
We'll be right back, and now back to the show.
我们马上回来，现在回到节目中。

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.769
There were many times we were in big group scenes, whether it was in the conference room.
有很多次我们都在拍大型群戏，不管是在会议室里。

00:12:39.769 --> 00:12:45.129
You know, we had so many conference room scenes, and everyone would have a line and be bouncing around.
你知道的，我们有那么多的会议室场景，每个人都有一句台词，并且互动活跃。

00:12:45.129 --> 00:12:45.906
fun to watch.
看起来很有趣。

00:12:45.906 --> 00:12:52.593
You would feel that energy of all these people just like bringing their best game and feeding off of each other.
你会感受到那种能量，所有人都在发挥出最佳状态并互相启发。

00:12:52.593 --> 00:12:54.981
And I miss a day in the conference room.
我很怀念在会议室的那一天。

00:12:54.981 --> 00:12:57.429
Jenna, can you believe I'd ever say that?
珍娜（Jenna），你敢相信我会这么说吗？

00:12:57.429 --> 00:12:59.220
Because we spent so much time.
因为我们花了那么多时间。

00:12:59.220 --> 00:12:59.757
No, same.
不，我也是。

00:12:59.757 --> 00:13:01.370
If I could go back in time.
如果我能回到过去。

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:06.979
You know, I didn't become an actor because I wanted to be a star.
你知道的，我成为演员不是因为我想成为明星。

00:13:06.979 --> 00:13:15.850
I became an actor because was the place I felt most personally expressed and accepted was in my theater club in high school.
我成为演员是因为我觉得个人表达最充分、最被接受的地方，是我在高中时的戏剧俱乐部。

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:25.409
It was being with the other theater nerds and the people who just love diving into character and being funny and performing.
是和其他戏剧迷在一起，和那些热爱深入角色、搞笑和表演的人在一起。

00:13:25.409 --> 00:13:30.169
Like there was an ensemble aspect to why I loved being an actor.
我喜欢当演员有一个群戏方面的因素。

00:13:30.169 --> 00:13:40.450
And so that was part of what drove me to LA was I wanted to find my acting troupe And my acting troupe, I hoped, would be a TV show.
所以这也是驱使我来到洛杉矶的一部分原因，我想找到我的演员剧团，而我希望我的演员剧团能成为一个电视剧剧组。

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:43.490
Did you guys know anybody in LA when you moved out to LA?
你们搬到洛杉矶时，在洛杉矶认识什么人吗？

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:46.210
Did you just move out completely cold, more or less?
你几乎是完全盲目地就搬过去了？

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:48.409
I didn't know a single person.
我一个人都不认识。

00:13:48.409 --> 00:13:49.850
I knew two people.
我认识两个人。

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:59.529
So this guy I had gone to college with and studied theater with, he had gone out the year prior, and I moved in with him.
和我一起上大学、学戏剧的一个家伙，他前一年去了那边，然后我就跟他合租。

00:13:59.529 --> 00:14:05.330
We were not boyfriend and girlfriend, but so I knew a guy, and he had a commercial agent.
我们不是男女朋友，但总之我认识这么个人，他有一个商业经纪人。

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:08.049
So I was pretty sure, probably, you know, right?
所以我当时挺确定的，可能吧，你知道的，对吧？

00:14:08.049 --> 00:14:12.649
Like his commercial agent would be my commercial agent, obviously, right?
就像他的商业经纪人理所当然也会成为我的商业经纪人，对吧？

00:14:12.649 --> 00:14:15.809
You got up on my holiday and you said, give me a commercial agent.
你过节的时候突然跑来跟我说，给我找个商业经纪人。

00:14:15.809 --> 00:14:16.649
Yes.
没错。

00:14:16.649 --> 00:14:17.809
That's the same as this guy.
这就跟这家伙的情况一样。

00:14:17.809 --> 00:14:21.970
So as I'm driving out, I know I have a commercial agent already.
所以当我开车赶过去的时候，我知道自己已经有一个商业经纪人了。

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.649
They just haven't met me yet.
他们只是还没见过我罢了。

00:14:23.649 --> 00:14:32.610
And then also, my mom had a friend at church whose son wasn't a literary agent at William Morris.
另外，我妈妈在教堂有个朋友，她儿子是威廉莫里斯的文学经纪人。

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:42.154
And I was pretty sure he was going to rep me across the board, you know, just um, again I didn't know that literary agents don't handle actors.
我当时非常确信他会全面代理我，你知道的，就是嗯……再说一次，我那时候根本不知道文学经纪人不带演员。

00:14:42.154 --> 00:15:24.269
So those were the two people I met and um Neither of those leads panned out That's that's who I knew do you guys ever have I have this thing as now that I'm older I have this thing where I miss my youthful naivete a little bit like I miss the part of me that would like like load up a car and drive to LA or shout from a, you know, a mountainside about, and I still have versions of, you know, things I'm really going for and things I'm really envisioning but it's almost like when you know a lot more, there's a lot more that you can be afraid of or there's a lot more, you almost realize like, oh, it's hard to get an acting job.
所以我见的就是这两个人，而且，呃……这两个线索后来都没成。这就是我认识的人。你们有没有过——我现在年纪大了，有时会有这种感觉——我会有点怀念自己年轻时的天真，比如我怀念曾经那个会把车塞满、一路开车去洛杉矶，或者在山坡上大喊大叫的自己。虽然我现在对于自己真正追求和设想的事情，依然有各种版本的坚持，但几乎就是当你懂得更多的时候，你所害怕的东西也就更多了，或者说你会猛然意识到，哦，找个演员的工作真难。

00:15:24.269 --> 00:15:26.669
Like, it's hard to get these jobs.
就像，想得到这些工作太难了。

00:15:26.669 --> 00:15:29.830
And I would just waltz into some of these rooms.
而我以前就会大摇大摆地走进某些试镜房间。

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:34.830
It was like with so much weird bravado, like so much weird 26-year-old bravado.
就带着一种无比诡异的虚张声势，26岁年轻人那种莫名其妙的虚张声势。

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.509
And just be like, this is my role, you know it, right?
然后直接说：这就是我的角色，你懂的，对吧？

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:39.350
You know, that was my energy.
你知道的，我当时就是这种气场。

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:42.470
And sometimes they'd be like, yeah, it is, you know?
有时他们会说：对啊，确实是，你知道吗？

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.669
Yeah, yeah.
对，没错。

00:15:43.669 --> 00:15:46.870
It's true though, it is, what is that phrase?
不过真的，就是那个词怎么说来着？

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:47.870
Youth is wasted on the young.
青春转瞬即逝，白白浪费在了年轻人身上。

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:48.830
Waste it on the young, yeah.
可不就是浪费在年轻人身上嘛。

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:49.830
Yeah.
对啊。

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:53.190
We, my, I went and did an improv show at Harvard.
我们，我，我之前去哈佛做过一场即兴喜剧秀。

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:56.110
They invited me as their guest for their show.
他们邀请我作为嘉宾去演出。

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:57.110
It's so fun.
太好玩了。

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:01.590
And you're just around all these kids that are just at the beginning, you know?
你身边都是那些刚刚起步的孩子，你知道吗？

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:15.509
And I took my daughter and my son with me because they, at the time they were freshmen in high school and we go, and we're walking around Harvard and my son looks at me who has not signed up for any extracurricular or anything.
我把女儿和儿子也带上了，因为他们当时上高一。我们去那儿，在哈佛校园里闲逛，结果我那个根本没报名参加任何课外活动或什么的儿子看着我。

00:16:15.509 --> 00:16:17.330
He's like, yeah, I could go here.
他说：这地方我也能考进来。

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:18.169
Yeah.
对啊。

00:16:18.169 --> 00:16:19.269
And I'm like, okay, buddy.
我就想，好吧，老兄。

00:16:19.269 --> 00:16:28.350
Well, you might want to sign up for some stuff, but it's that thing, Josh, where you're like, you walk in a room like, yeah, this job is mine.
嗯，你可能还是得去报点活动什么的。但就是那种感觉，乔希，就像你走进一个房间会觉得：没错，这工作就是我的。

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:29.190
whatever.
随便吧。

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:33.429
The idea of waltzing into that room and auditioning for a pilot.
一想到要大摇大摆地走进那个房间去为一个试播集试镜。

00:16:33.429 --> 00:16:37.950
Like Josh was our first audition day one of day one of auditions.
就像乔希是我们试镜第一天的第一个试镜者。

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:38.789
Like he walked in.
他当时就这么走了进去。

00:16:38.789 --> 00:16:39.870
Oh, you told that story.
哦，你讲过这个故事。

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:41.350
Yes, I forgot that.
对，我都给忘了。

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:43.429
It was crazy.
太疯狂了。

00:16:43.429 --> 00:16:47.870
It was just the universe kind of wanted him to do this part.
感觉就像是命运之神某种程度上就想让他演这个角色。

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.789
You guys had been trying to find that moment.
你们之前一直在努力寻找那个时刻。听起来在《办公室》找到你之前，或者说在你找到《办公室》之前的八年、十年里，当你走进去并且发现这正是绝配时，确实有一种很奇妙的感觉，对吧？

00:16:50.789 --> 00:17:00.509
It sounds like for eight, 10 years before you found the office, before the office found you, there is something kind of magical about when you walk in and it's the right fit, right?
就像你，然后你加入了这个演员阵容，你必须表现得好像你们已经认识彼此很多年了一样。

00:17:00.509 --> 00:17:06.430
Like you, and then you're on this cast and you have to figure out how to act like you've all known each other for all these years.
你们两个能聊聊吗？因为我们之前讨论过那种想法，比如乔希被选中饰演杰森·席格尔的死党——而他之前并不认识杰森，也不认识艾莉森·海迪根。

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:15.630
Can you guys both talk about, because we've talked about that idea of like, Josh got cast and had to play the best friend of Jason Segal who he didn't know, and Alison Haddigan, who he didn't know.
你们能聊聊那种感觉吗？你走进去，得到了这个机会，然后你们要伪造出一种你们已经在一起经历了许多年的历史感。

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:21.910
Like can you talk about like you walk in, you get this opportunity and then you forge this history that you've all been together for all these years.
你们能稍微谈谈当时是怎么处理的，那个时刻是如何找到你们的吗？

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:26.390
Can you just talk about like a little bit about how you approached that, how that moment found you?
好吧，我来插一句，我觉得自己非常幸运，因为我认识奥斯卡·努涅斯和凯特·弗兰纳里。

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:33.829
Well, I will jump in here and say that I was very fortunate because I knew Oscar Nunez and Kate Flannery.
现在，我要说的是。

00:17:33.829 --> 00:17:36.029
Now, let me say this.
我们当时并不知道对方也被选中了，因为我们的剧开播时社交媒体还没那么发达，伙计们。

00:17:36.029 --> 00:17:43.029
We didn't know the other had been cast because our show took off before social media, you guys.
当时大家不会把自己的所有动态都发到网上。

00:17:43.109 --> 00:17:46.390
There was like, people weren't posting what all they were doing.
对，我们什么都不知道。

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:47.710
Yeah, we didn't know anything.
我们对一切一无所知。

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:48.549
We didn't know anything.
所以我走到片场，然后看到了奥斯卡，我和他曾经一起做过素描喜剧秀。

00:17:48.549 --> 00:17:54.109
So I walked onto set and there's Oscar and he and I had done a sketch comedy show together.
我们在演员工会剧团（The Groundlings）认识的。

00:17:54.109 --> 00:17:57.690
We had met at the Groundlings And I was like, oh my gosh, Oscar.
当时我就想：哦天哪，奥斯卡！

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:03.009
And not only what did I know him, but he was my desk mate in my club.
不仅我认识他，而且他在我的俱乐部里还是我的同桌。

00:18:03.009 --> 00:18:06.529
So I was like, okay, we're gonna be off to the races here.
所以我当时就觉得：好吧，我们这下稳了。

00:18:06.529 --> 00:18:12.450
And then Kate Flannery walked on set and she and I had done an improv show together called Bitch Planet.
然后凯特·弗兰纳里也走上了片场，我和她一起做过一个叫“碧池星球”（Bitch Planet）的即兴喜剧秀。

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.049
And I was like, Kate, what the heck?
当时我就想：凯特，搞什么鬼？

00:18:15.049 --> 00:18:21.369
So I was so tickled that little corner, I knew some people, but yeah.
所以我超级开心，在那个小角落里，我认识一些人，不过也仅此而已。

00:18:21.369 --> 00:18:26.930
But Jenna, you had to like have this sort of shared history with John, who you had only just met.
但詹娜，你得跟约翰建立那种共同的历史感，而你们明明才刚认识不久。

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:33.529
Well, we did do on-camera screen tests as our final network test for the office.
对，作为《办公室》最终的电视台试镜，我们确实做过镜头前的银幕测试。

00:18:33.529 --> 00:18:40.730
And so I spent two days being mixed and matched with different dweights and Michaels and gyms.
所以我花了整整两天时间，跟不同的 Dwight（德怀特）、Michael（迈克尔）和 Jim（吉姆）进行各种组合搭配。

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:48.829
And I knew immediately that John needed to be gym if I was gonna be Pam and I knew I was Pam.
我立刻就明白，如果我要演 Pam（帕姆），那 Jim 就必须是约翰，而且我知道我就是帕姆。

00:18:48.829 --> 00:18:50.589
I knew it deep.
我内心深处非常确信这一点。

00:18:50.589 --> 00:18:58.950
I never had this kind of delusional confidence that we're talking about where I was like, this is mine until Pam.
我从来没有过我们刚才提到的那种妄自尊大的自信，直到遇到帕姆这个角色前，我从没觉得“这就是我的”。

00:18:58.950 --> 00:18:59.910
Yeah.
对。

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.789
But I knew she was mine.
但我知道她就是属于我的。

00:19:01.789 --> 00:19:14.829
And like to the point where there was another actress in the waiting room, and she had on these, like thigh-high suede boots that she was wearing, and I thought Pam would never wear those boots.
甚至夸张到，当时候诊室里还有另一个女演员，她穿着一双那种过膝的麂皮靴子，而我想着帕姆绝对不会穿那种靴子。

00:19:14.829 --> 00:19:17.470
That's not Pam.
那才不是帕姆。

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:20.269
Pam would never wear those boots.
帕姆绝不会穿那种靴子。

00:19:20.269 --> 00:19:22.789
I know Pam, she doesn't dress like that.
我了解帕姆，她才不那样穿衣打扮。

00:19:22.789 --> 00:19:42.460
So, yeah, but then when I, so after that, on the second day, you know, I didn't know really where I stood, but they were matching me with John a lot, but they weren't matching me with Rain a lot and everybody knew he was gonna be Dwight.
所以，是的，不过后来当我在第二天时，你知道的，我其实不太确定自己的处境，他们把我跟约翰配对了很多次，但没有把我跟雷恩配对很多次，而所有人都知道他会演德怀特。

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.660
Like it was, we're like that guy's Dwight and so I got nervous.
就像当时的情况是，我们都觉得那家伙就是德怀特，所以我开始紧张了。

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:51.619
I was like, well, I think they're matching me with who I think is Jim, but they're not matching me with who I think is Dwight.
我在想：好吧，我觉得他们把我跟我认为是吉姆的人配对，但他们没有把我跟饰演出我认为是德怀特的人配对。

00:19:51.619 --> 00:19:57.700
And then on the, so on the second day, John leaned in and he said, you're my favorite Pam.
然后到了第二天，约翰凑过来对我说：你是我最喜欢的帕姆。

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.779
And I said, you're my favorite Jim, you're my favorite Jim.
我说：你是我最喜欢的吉姆，你是我最喜欢的吉姆。

00:20:00.779 --> 00:20:01.859
But we agree.
我们俩不谋而合。

00:20:01.859 --> 00:20:06.220
And then when my agent called me and told me I got the role, I said, but who's Jim?
后来当我的经纪人打电话告诉我得到了这个角色时，我说：那吉姆是谁？

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.900
And they said, it's John Krasinski.
他们说：是约翰·卡拉辛斯基。

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:09.220
And I said, oh, thank God.
我说：哦，谢天谢地。

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.579
And they said, that's what he said.
他们说，他也是这么说的。

00:20:11.579 --> 00:20:14.619
They said, she said, who's Pam?
他们说，他问：帕姆是谁？

00:20:14.619 --> 00:20:16.099
And he said, Jenna Fisher.
然后他说：是詹娜·费舍尔。

00:20:16.099 --> 00:20:17.619
And he said, oh, thank God.
然后他说：哦，谢天谢地。

00:20:17.619 --> 00:20:27.220
So we did have a connection from the audition that folded very easily into the life of the show.
所以我们在试镜时确实就有一种默契，这种默契非常自然地融入到了剧集的生命中。

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:28.840
There's like magic that goes into this.
这里面确实包含着某种魔法。

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:29.380
It really is.
真的如此。

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:35.299
You know, I went through that process with Kobe Smolders at the studio, at the network.
你知道的，我当年在电视台也经历过科比·斯莫德斯的那个选角过程。

00:20:35.299 --> 00:20:38.819
And by the time, I think we were the only people you guys brought to the network.
到最后，我觉得我们可能是你们带去电视台的仅有的人选。

00:20:38.819 --> 00:20:40.980
There were other actors at the studio, but you only brought us.
制片厂还有其他演员，但你们只带了我们。

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:51.220
So by the time you end up on set, after having been through the audition process, which is such a heightened, strange, disorienting thing, you actually do feel like you know the person.
所以当你们经历完试镜流程后最终来到片场时——这种试镜本身就是一种高度紧张、陌生且让人不知所措的过程——你真的会觉得自己认识这个人。

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:56.980
You know, you've stared into their eyes lovingly, like, enough that there's some kind of bond developed.
你知道的，你们深情地凝视过彼此的眼睛，这种程度已经建立起某种纽带了。

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:59.059
In front of executives in a small room.
在一小间屋子里当着高管们的面。

00:20:59.059 --> 00:20:59.619
Yeah.
对。

00:20:59.619 --> 00:21:02.180
I was thinking about our two pilots, the two moments that we launched.
我当时在想我们那两个试播集，也就是我们起航的两个时刻。

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:05.059
How much your mother had zero expectations, right?
你母亲当时是多么地毫无期待啊，对吧？

00:21:05.059 --> 00:21:08.660
We were this young weird little show on CBS.
我们当时只是CBS电视台一部年轻而又古怪的小众剧集。

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:10.819
We didn't think we were going to get picked up.
我们当时觉得我们不会被选中。

00:21:10.819 --> 00:21:13.140
We thought the wrong network bought us.
我们以为买下我们的那家电视台搞错了。

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.203
we should be on NBC.
我们本来应该去NBC的。

00:21:14.203 --> 00:21:17.342
We thought we should be on NBC like with the office, right?
我们觉得我们应该像《办公室》那样去NBC，对吧？

00:21:17.342 --> 00:21:19.789
You guys like premiere the year before we did.
你们首播的那年比我们早一年。

00:21:19.789 --> 00:21:21.438
Oh no, NBC didn't buy the show.
哦不，NBC并没有买下这部剧。

00:21:21.438 --> 00:21:27.184
We sold to CBS that the shows there are different from the show we're trying to do with their older skewing.
我们卖给了CBS，那里的节目和我们想做的节目不同，他们的受众年龄偏大。

00:21:27.184 --> 00:21:28.407
It's not going to work.
这行不通。

00:21:28.407 --> 00:21:32.450
And no, there was no expectation at any point in pop culture about our show.
而且不，在流行文化中，大家对我们的节目没有任何期待。

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:39.472
Our show like snuck through and somehow got on the air because CBS happened to be looking for a younger skewing show at that moment.
我们的节目就像是偷偷摸摸、莫名其妙地播出了，因为CBS当时刚好在寻找一部受众更年轻的节目。

00:21:39.472 --> 00:21:42.238
So we just were like stealth flying under the radar.
所以我们就像是在雷达底下隐形飞行。

00:21:42.238 --> 00:21:44.420
That's that's how how much mother got on.
老妈就是这样播出的。

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:47.940
The office, I think about this when I think about you guys.
《办公室》，每当我想起你们时我都会想到这一点。

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:58.099
How daunting was this expectation that you're taking this British show that was this very revered, and especially among comedy nerds in America, right?
你们要翻拍这部在英国备受推崇、尤其是受到美国喜剧迷们追捧的英国剧集，这种期待带给你们的压力有多大？

00:21:58.099 --> 00:22:04.180
Like this very revered piece that's very different from what your show was gonna be in a great way.
就好像是一部备受尊崇的作品，而且以一种很棒的方式，它和你们即将制作的节目非常不同。

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:06.500
You guys carved out your own territory.
你们开辟了自己的领地。

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.660
How much pressure did you feel launching a show?
推出一部剧时，你们感到了多大的压力？

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:11.819
Because there's so much pressure trying to launch a show on Thursday night at NBC.
因为在NBC周四晚上推出一部剧本身就有巨大的压力。

00:22:11.819 --> 00:22:19.740
And then you have this extra pressure of trying to live up and or differentiate from this thing that a lot of comedy people thought was the new Bible.
然后你还要承受额外的压力，试图达到或超越很多喜剧迷视为新圣经的那部作品，或者与之划清界限。

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:20.579
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:22:20.579 --> 00:22:23.980
It's with like the reverence for that British office, rightfully so.
人们对英版《办公室》有着那种敬意，这是理所当然的。

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:26.579
It's a great show, very different show from your show.
那是一部伟大的剧，和你们的剧非常不同。

00:22:26.579 --> 00:22:27.500
What did that feel like?
那是什么感觉？

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.460
Cause we didn't work under any kind of expectation or pressure.
因为我们在开播时没有任何期待或压力。

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:37.019
There were multiple articles written before we ever premiered about how shitty we were.
在我们开播之前，已经有好几篇文章在写我们有多烂了。

00:22:37.019 --> 00:22:38.380
Before anyone saw it.
在任何人看之前。

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:45.980
Like multiple television critics wrote articles about how they never wanted to watch our version.
就像好几位电视评论家写文章说他们永远不想看我们的版本。

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:52.420
I have a scrapbook full of these things because I was, so, you know, I was like physical articles.
我有一个贴满这些东西的剪贴簿，因为当时，你知道的，都是些实体文章。

00:22:52.420 --> 00:23:08.220
I remember I was on the tube in London and there was like the London TV Guide and it had pictures of the original cast members and who was playing their counterparts and they were just ripping us to shreds.
我记得我当时在伦敦的地铁上，那里有类似《伦敦电视指南》的东西，上面有原版演员的照片，还有谁在扮演他们的对应角色，他们把我们贬得一文不值。

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:09.500
And we hadn't even come out yet.
而我们当时甚至都还没开播。

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:10.500
You've done nothing wrong.
你们什么都没做错。

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:11.500
Nothing had even happened yet.
当时甚至什么都还没发生。

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.099
They hadn't even seen what we were gonna do.
他们甚至还没看到我们要拍成什么样。

00:23:14.099 --> 00:23:20.420
And so, yeah, we were up against, like, people were just ready to hate on us.
所以，是的，我们面对的是——人们已经准备好讨厌我们了。

00:23:20.420 --> 00:23:21.420
Yeah.
是的。

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:22.900
What a weird extra pressure.
多么奇怪的额外压力啊。

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:24.740
Angela, how did you experience that?
安吉拉，你是怎么经历这一切的？

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:25.740
That seems impossible.
这听起来太不可思议了。

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:29.819
It's already such a hard job to launch a TV show and then to have that shadow.
推出一部电视剧本来就已经是一项极其艰难的工作了，还要背负那样的阴影。

00:23:29.819 --> 00:23:30.819
It's a lot.
这压力太大了。

00:23:30.819 --> 00:23:31.819
It's a lot.
压力太大了。

00:23:31.819 --> 00:23:35.539
And also, I mean, the majority of the people on the show were also comedy nerds.
而且，我的意思是，剧组的大多数人也都是喜剧迷。

00:23:35.539 --> 00:23:41.779
So we knew, we'd watch the BBC version and we were like, that's pretty damn perfect, like holy crap.
所以我们知道，我们看过BBC版，我们当时觉得，这简直完美得不像话，天哪。

00:23:41.779 --> 00:23:42.779
Right.
没错。

00:23:42.779 --> 00:23:43.779
How do we not be that?
我们怎么才能不变成那样呢？

00:23:43.779 --> 00:23:46.980
Yeah, we don't want to tread on that or not do it justice.
对，我们不想步其后尘，也不想毁了这部经典。

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:54.220
So I think we were carrying that pressure of like, oh wow, please, please let us be able to do something great with it.
所以我想我们当时背负着那种压力，就觉得，哦天哪，求求你，让我们也能用它拍出好作品吧。

00:23:54.220 --> 00:24:04.809
And then I remember when we were filming Diversity Day, I remember thinking, wow, okay, we have got something really special.
然后我记得我们在拍《多元化日》时，我记得当时想，哇，好吧，我们拍出了真正特别的东西。

00:24:04.809 --> 00:24:08.490
I think the pressure from the pilot had been lifted a little bit.
我觉得首播集带来的压力稍微减轻了一点。

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:10.130
Right, get the pilot out of the way.
没错，把首播集熬过去了。

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:11.369
Are you talking about episode two now?
你现在是在说第二集吗？

00:24:11.369 --> 00:24:12.730
Episode two is diversity day?
第二集是《多元化日》？

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:15.490
Yeah, I should say episode two of season one.
对，我应该说的是第一季的第二集。

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:20.529
And I felt like, okay, we could maybe do our spin on this and do it really well.
当时我觉得，好吧，我们也许能用自己的方式来演绎，而且能演得很好。

00:24:20.529 --> 00:24:23.329
This is a group of crazy talented people.
这是一群才华横溢到疯狂的人。

00:24:23.329 --> 00:24:25.410
And I got hopeful in diversity day.
在拍《多元化日》时我开始感到希望了。

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:28.450
The pilot was just this pressure cooker of like, oh my gosh.
首播集简直就像一个压力锅，天哪。

00:24:28.450 --> 00:24:29.269
I am challenging.
我正在迎接挑战。

00:24:29.269 --> 00:24:35.529
And the pilot was the same script as the British pilot, But Diversity Day was original.
首播集的剧本和英版首播集一样，但《多元化日》是原创的。

00:24:35.529 --> 00:24:42.969
And speaking of being like young and writing a hit television show, Diversity Day was written by B.J.
说到年轻并写出热门电视剧，《多元化日》是由B.J.

00:24:42.969 --> 00:24:46.210
Novak who I believe was like 26 at the time.
诺瓦克编剧的，我记得他当时大概26岁。

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.369
Yeah, and it's a beautiful piece of television.
是的，这是一部极佳的电视作品。

00:24:49.369 --> 00:24:50.529
It's a great episode.
这是一集伟大的剧。

00:24:50.529 --> 00:24:51.769
Yeah, great episode.
对，太棒的一集了。

00:24:51.769 --> 00:25:04.849
And so yeah, I think when we, in the production process, when we started doing those episodes that were our scripts and not the British scripts, like not just redoing the British scripts.
所以，是的，我觉得当我们在制作过程中开始拍摄那些属于我们自己的剧本、而不是英版剧本，不是仅仅重制英版剧本时——

00:25:04.849 --> 00:25:10.650
And, you know, Greg Daniels did not want to redo the British pilot.
而且，你知道的，格雷格·丹尼尔斯并不想重制英版的首播集。

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:17.410
He wanted to write an original pilot, but NBC was like, no, we know that's a hit.
他想写一个原创的首播集，但NBC说，不，我们知道那是爆款。

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:21.049
So you have to do, for the pilot, you have to do that.
所以对于首播集，你们必须拍那个。

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:28.529
But then when we got picked up after the pilot, then they said, okay, you can write five original ones to go with it.
但后来我们在首播集之后被续订了，他们就说，好吧，你们可以写五集原创的来配合它。

00:25:28.529 --> 00:25:41.611
You know, it also occurs to me that it's great to be an underdog, like it's hard to be an underdog, but it's better to be an underdog because, yeah, when people have too many expectations of you, it can be a little crushing.
你知道，我忽然也觉得，当弱者挺好的，虽然当弱者很难，但当弱者更好，因为，对啊，当人们对你抱有太多期望时，可能会有点压得人喘不过气来。

00:25:41.611 --> 00:25:47.567
But if they have zero expectations or very low expectations, you can always come in and surprise them.
但如果他们对你零期望或期望很低，你随时都可以出其不意地惊艳他们。

00:25:47.567 --> 00:25:48.443
I was watching.
我当时在看。

00:25:48.443 --> 00:25:53.057
My wife and I were watching the, did you guys see the Simone Biles documentary?
我和我太太在看那个，你们看过西蒙·拜尔斯的纪录片吗？

00:25:53.057 --> 00:25:54.049
No, it's amazing.
没有，太精彩了。

00:25:54.049 --> 00:25:54.724
It's great.
很好看。

00:25:54.724 --> 00:25:55.224
Yeah.
对。

00:25:55.224 --> 00:25:59.763
I mean, it's a series, but she said there's nothing better than coming up.
我的意思是，这是一部系列片，但她说没有什么比默默崛起更好的了。

00:25:59.569 --> 00:26:02.527
Like like she didn't like being at the top, top, top.
就好像她不喜欢一直处在最顶峰、最顶峰。

00:26:02.527 --> 00:26:05.485
She liked being where did this girl come from, right?
她喜欢那种“这个女孩是从哪儿冒出来的”的感觉，对吧？

00:26:05.485 --> 00:26:05.985
Yeah.
对。

00:26:05.985 --> 00:26:11.231
And I said to my wife, I said, I wonder if subconsciously, remember when she got the twisties?
我对太太说，我在想，是不是潜意识里——还记得她患上“空中迷失”的时候吗？

00:26:11.231 --> 00:26:11.731
Yes.
记得。

00:26:11.731 --> 00:26:14.633
Like, and she had to put withdraw from the Olympics.
就像那样，她不得不退出奥运会。

00:26:14.633 --> 00:26:20.996
I was like, yeah, I wonder if she unconsciously gave herself a restart where she had to come back from the bottom.
我就在想，是的，我在想她是不是无意识地给了自己一个重新开始的机会，让她必须从谷底爬上来。

00:26:20.996 --> 00:26:23.507
Like, She loved the climb up against so much.
就像，她太喜欢那种逆流而上的攀登了。

00:26:23.009 --> 00:26:24.410
That's a great theory.
这真是一个绝佳的推论。

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:27.529
She needed to be an underdog again to have that hunger.
她需要再次成为弱者，才能重新拥有那种渴望。

00:26:27.529 --> 00:26:29.930
Because it's like heavy as the head that wears the crown.
因为欲戴王冠，必承其重嘛。

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:30.769
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:26:30.769 --> 00:26:32.289
Like it's hard to be at the top.
处于顶峰是很艰难的。

00:26:32.289 --> 00:26:38.809
And I think like if you talk to anyone at the top, top, top, they'll be, they'll be wistful for the early days.
我想，如果你和任何站在最顶峰的人交流，他们都会对早期日子充满留恋。

00:26:38.809 --> 00:26:47.329
They'll be wistful for the days when they were just like shouting into, you know, the Hollywood girls and, and, you know, living on the couch, right?
他们会怀念那些日子，那时他们只是在好莱坞的喧嚣中大声呼喊，住在沙发上，对吧？

00:26:47.329 --> 00:26:52.769
I also sort of look back on it now with great pride that we weren't on a single billboard or a bus.
我现在回想起来也感到非常自豪，我们当时甚至没有一块广告牌或一辆公交车广告。

00:26:52.769 --> 00:26:57.529
Like you could drive around LA and never know that we were gonna be on NBC.
你开车转遍洛杉矶，根本不会知道我们要上NBC。

00:26:57.529 --> 00:26:59.690
And there was another show, and Jan, I forget the name of it.
当时还有另一部剧，简，我忘了叫什么名字了。

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:01.289
It was My Name is Earl?
是《愚人善事》吗？

00:27:01.289 --> 00:27:02.490
No, no, no.
不，不，不。

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:08.210
There was another show that was gonna launch the same week as us, and they were on every bus and everything.
还有另一部剧和我们同一周首播，他们到处都是公交车广告和宣传。

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:18.490
No, my name is Earl launch the year we did, and from the same studio and got so much more promotion and press, we felt like we were very much in the shadow of that giant mustache on Earl's face.
不，《愚人善事》和我们同一年开播，来自同一个制片厂，获得了多得多的宣传和报道，我们觉得自己完全笼罩在厄尔脸上那撮巨大胡子的阴影之下了。

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:20.369
We're always looking up at the birds.
我们总是只能仰望那些大人物。

00:27:20.369 --> 00:27:28.210
We all clearly have been in the shadow of the, my name is Earl, the first one that came to mind for me.
我们显然都曾笼罩在别人的阴影下，我脑子里想到的第一个就是《愚人善事》。

00:27:28.289 --> 00:27:29.529
We felt very inferior.
我们当时感到非常自卑。

00:27:29.529 --> 00:27:31.170
We were the underdog to that one.
跟那部剧比起来，我们就像是弱者。

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:32.369
But isn't it amazing?
但这不是挺神奇的吗？

00:27:32.369 --> 00:27:40.210
I mean, there's certain shows that are popular nowadays where you're like, the algorithm and the powers that be are making this a thing.
我是说，现在有些剧很受欢迎，你会觉得，算法和高层在刻意把它们炒作起来。

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:44.130
Whereas our shows are both powered by fans finding them.
而我们的剧全靠粉丝自己去发掘。

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:49.569
Like it's literally like old, tiny word of mouth kind of classic stuff, right?
就真的像是那种老派、小范围的口碑相传的经典作品，对吧？

00:27:49.569 --> 00:27:56.450
Well, Josh, the closest we came to having to subvert any kind of expectation was we knew we didn't want to be too close to friends.
嗯，乔许，我们离颠覆任何预期最近的一次，就是我们知道自己不想太像《老友记》。

00:27:56.450 --> 00:28:00.009
That was the one shadow that it's kind of akin to you guys doing the office.
那是那种类似你们拍《办公室》时会遇到的阴影。

00:28:00.009 --> 00:28:10.490
Like we were in the, we were definitely were aware that 7,000 shows had tried to be a friends-like show between when friends launched and when our show launched in 2005.
就像我们当时……在《老友记》开播到我们2005年开播之间，我们绝对清楚有7000部剧都想模仿《老友记》。

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:19.090
And I'd say that's, I realize I do have some kind connection to the feeling you guys were describing of, I know we're going to get compared to this other show.
我想说的是——我意识到我确实对你们刚才描述的那种感觉有所共鸣，就是“我知道我们会被拿去和另一部剧作比较”。

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:23.256
How do we make sure we are as different from that show in the content, right?
我们如何确保我们在内容上和那部剧尽可能不同呢？

00:28:23.256 --> 00:28:27.908
Maybe the billboard, if we ever, we didn't get too many billboards either, by the way.
也许广告牌——如果我们曾有过的话，顺便说一句，我们也没多少广告牌。

00:28:27.908 --> 00:28:40.295
But maybe the cast photo looks similar, but when you turn on the show, how do we make sure the show feels molecularly different on kind of this like DNA level, like this show has a different rhythm, a different tone from friends.
但也许演员合照看起来差不多，不过当你打开这部剧时，我们如何确保这部剧在某种DNA层面上感觉从分子结构上就完全不同？比如这部剧的节奏和基调都和《老友记》不同。

00:28:40.295 --> 00:28:42.080
And that was part of our mission.
这也是我们的使命之一。

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:45.705
And I'm sure it sounds like that was part of your guys mission too.
而且我敢肯定，听起来这似乎也是你们的使命之一。

00:28:45.705 --> 00:28:47.544
Like we have to make this our own.
就像我们必须走出自己的风格。

00:28:47.544 --> 00:28:53.007
You said you didn't exhale Angela until episode two when you were like, this is us making it our own.
安吉拉，你说过直到第二集你才松了一口气，当时你觉得：这才是我们自己拍的东西。

00:28:53.007 --> 00:28:55.279
This is actually what it's supposed to be.
这才是它本来该有的样子。

00:28:55.279 --> 00:28:58.850
So yeah, so you guys watched or re-watched rather our pilot, yeah?
所以，好吧，你们看了或者说重温了我们的试播集，是吗？

00:28:59.490 --> 00:28:59.990
Yes.
是的。

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:03.240
You guys asked us, what did you ask us overall reactions?
你们问过我们……你们问了我们什么？整体反应吗？

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.121
Was that the phrase you guys use?
是你们用的那个词吗？

00:29:04.849 --> 00:29:06.353
Yes, overall reactions.
对，整体反应。

00:29:06.353 --> 00:29:09.490
Please, we'd love to hear your overall reaction.
请吧，我们很想听听你们的整体反应。

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:10.160
Oh, man.
哦，天哪。

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:11.083
I loved it.
我太喜欢了。

00:29:11.083 --> 00:29:19.134
I hadn't seen this in a long, long time, and I forgot the different beats of it, and I loved it.
我好久好久没看过这个了，我都忘了里面的各种节奏，但我真的好喜欢。

00:29:19.134 --> 00:29:21.567
And Josh sounds really weird.
而且乔许看起来真的很搞笑。

00:29:21.567 --> 00:29:24.921
Sorry, but I'm so like smitten with Ted.
抱歉，但我真的被特德迷住了。

00:29:24.921 --> 00:29:27.773
I'm just like, Ted is so charming.
我只想说，特德太有魅力了。

00:29:27.773 --> 00:29:29.870
I was so rooting for Ted.
我当时太支持特德了。

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:32.050
This is what I wrote down.
这是我写下来的。

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:45.805
I said the scene in the bar with Ted describing his future wedding to the woman he just met, followed by the look on his face when he sees Robin for the first time.
我说，酒吧里特德向刚认识的女人描述他未来的婚礼的那场戏，紧接着是他第一次看到罗宾时的表情。

00:29:45.805 --> 00:29:50.166
This is 100% when every woman fell in love with Ted.
这绝对是所有女性爱上特德的时刻。

00:29:50.166 --> 00:29:50.666
Yes!
对！

00:29:50.666 --> 00:29:55.950
I'm like this, oh my God, where was this guy when I was dating?
我当时就像这样，天哪，我约会的时候这个男人在哪儿？

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:57.124
Where was Ted?
特德在哪儿？

00:29:57.124 --> 00:30:06.014
And I know this must, we've talked about this then, Josh, but this, every woman did fall in love with Ted.
我知道这一定……我们当时也讨论过这个，乔许，但每一个女性确实都爱上了特德。

00:30:05.849 --> 00:30:08.369
And then you have that, you gotta carry that around.
然后你得带着这个光环生活。

00:30:08.369 --> 00:30:11.009
I mean, John Krasinski had that with Jim.
我是说，约翰·卡拉辛斯基演吉姆时也是这样。

00:30:11.009 --> 00:30:14.170
I mean, for sure, here's when I was sunk.
我是说，真的，我当时彻底沦陷了。

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:24.289
I was sunk there, but then I mean, forget about it now, is when he was like, you know, look, obviously you can't tell a woman you just met you lover, but it sucks that you can't.
我在那里就沦陷了，然后——现在先别管这个——就是他说，你知道，听着，显然你不能对刚认识的女人说你爱她，但不能说真的很逊。

00:30:24.289 --> 00:30:25.569
I just love that.
我太喜欢这句台词了。

00:30:25.569 --> 00:30:30.329
I'm like, I would have been like, oh, okay, let's go, where's the church, let's do this.
我会觉得，哦，好吧，我们走吧，教堂在哪儿，我们把这事办了。

00:30:30.329 --> 00:30:36.170
If I can find the email where I asked you guys to be on the show, I think I said, will you just come on and compliment me for 20 to 30 minutes?
如果我能找到当初邀请你们上节目的那封邮件，我想我当时说的是：你们能来聊20到30分钟然后顺便夸夸我吗？

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:39.450
And you were like, of course, that's what we'll do.
而你们当时的反应是：当然，我们就打算这么干。

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:50.250
It's so funny, there's, you know that line where one of my favorite things from the pilot is where he's talking to Yasmeen, I think, and says, you know, it's not like I sit around and think about my wedding.
太搞笑了，有一个……你知道那句台词吗？我在试播集中最喜欢的情节之一，就是他和亚斯敏（我想是她）说话时，说：你知道的，我可不是那种会坐着幻想自己婚礼的人。

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.970
And then he goes into this long fantasy about his wedding.
然后他就开始了一段关于自己婚礼的长篇大论。

00:30:52.970 --> 00:30:55.809
And he says at the end, you know, bam no DJ.
他在最后说，你知道的，棒极了，没有DJ。

00:30:55.809 --> 00:30:57.609
People will dance, I'm not gonna worry about it.
大家都会跳舞，我不用担心这个。

00:30:57.609 --> 00:30:59.289
Yes, I love that.
对，我太喜欢这个了。

00:30:59.289 --> 00:31:04.049
I'm not gonna show you the full, but I named my corporation, People Will Dance.
我不打算给你们看全貌，但我把我的公司命名为了“大家都会跳舞（People Will Dance）”。

00:31:04.049 --> 00:31:05.450
Oh, based on that line.
哦，就是根据那句台词来的。

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:14.809
Based on that line because it became a kind of weird little catchphrase in my life where I would just, it was a way to comfort myself and say, don't worry about it.
就是根据那句台词，因为它成了我生活中的一种奇怪的小口头禅，每当我需要安慰自己时就会用它，告诉自己：别担心。

00:31:14.809 --> 00:31:16.369
Like people will dance.
就像“大家都会跳舞”一样。

00:31:16.369 --> 00:31:19.690
You don't have to stress in this moment.
此时此刻你不需要压力太大。

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:27.289
And I still, I've had people come up to me and show me their arms and they've gotten People Will Dance tattooed on their form.
我到现在，还有人走到我面前，把手臂露给我看，他们把“大家都会跳舞”纹在了前臂上。

00:31:27.289 --> 00:31:29.730
Do you guys see the tattoos, the office tattoos?
你们见过那些纹身吗？《办公室》的纹身？

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:30.569
Yes.
见过。

00:31:30.569 --> 00:31:37.009
that one of the most startling things about being on a show is not of how much mother tattoos it's it's start it's shocking.
参演一部剧最让人震惊的事情之一，就是有那么多关于老妈的纹身，这太惊人了。

00:31:37.009 --> 00:31:37.694
It's amazing.
这太神奇了。

00:31:37.694 --> 00:31:38.194
Thanks.
谢谢。

00:31:38.194 --> 00:31:39.354
Thank you to the fans.
谢谢粉丝们。

00:31:39.354 --> 00:31:40.829
Yeah, it's a big commitment.
对，这真的是个很大的承诺。

00:31:40.829 --> 00:31:45.308
When when Josh auditioned, he did Angela, he he his audition, he had like two scenes.
当……当乔许试镜的时候，安吉拉，他……他的试镜大概有两场戏。

00:31:45.308 --> 00:31:46.783
One of them was that speech.
其中一场就是那段独白。

00:31:46.783 --> 00:31:50.788
And he did that speech in the room with our casting director, me and Carter.
他在选角导演、我和卡特面前的房间里完成了那段独白。

00:31:50.788 --> 00:31:53.159
And I think maybe Pam Frime and our director.
我想可能还有帕姆·弗里姆和我们的导演。

00:31:53.159 --> 00:31:59.693
And at the end of it, our casting director, Megan Ramen, there was just a beat in the room because Josh crushed that speech.
在结束后，我们的选角导演梅根·拉曼，房间里突然安静了一会儿，因为乔许把那段独白演绝了。

00:31:59.693 --> 00:32:00.641
It was so perfect.
太完美了。

00:32:00.641 --> 00:32:09.441
It was exactly as it was was then shot weeks later, and there was a little silence while all of us were blown away, and Megan Brandman says to Josh, will you marry me?
和几周后实际拍摄时一模一样，大家都被震撼到了，现场一片沉默，接着梅根·布兰德曼对乔许说：你愿意嫁给我吗？

00:32:09.309 --> 00:32:12.569
And we're like, this shit worked.
我们就觉得：这招管用。

00:32:12.569 --> 00:32:13.890
And I did.
然后我真的这么做了。

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:18.450
And it did last, but I married the casting director.
而且确实持久，我娶了选角导演。

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:20.210
No, it ended terribly.
不，结局惨烈。

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:26.529
Josh, did you ever audition for Jim Halpert in the office audition process?
乔许，你曾经在《办公室》的选角过程中为吉姆·哈尔珀特试镜过吗？

00:32:26.529 --> 00:32:27.450
I did.
我试过。

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:28.849
You did, didn't it, you?
你试过，对吧？

00:32:28.849 --> 00:32:29.930
Oh, the shit.
哦，天哪。

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:32.930
I did, I almost said it, but it's true.
我试过，我差点没承认，但这是真的。

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:37.930
I did, I mean, I was, I auditioned for everything.
我试过，我是说，我什么都试镜过。

00:32:39.009 --> 00:32:39.849
Of course.
当然。

00:32:39.849 --> 00:32:40.690
Me too.
我也是。

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:42.450
I tested for Arrested Development.
我还为《发展受阻》试镜过。

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:43.289
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吧？

00:32:43.289 --> 00:32:54.230
I auditioned for everything and I remember, I auditioned for Jim and I didn't quite understand if he was smart or not smart.
我什么都试过，我记得我为吉姆试镜时，我不太弄得清他到底是聪明还是不聪明。

00:32:54.230 --> 00:33:08.950
Like there was, I was a little confused about whether he was part of the like smart gang or not sparking, and I remember the, I had a good audition, but my improvs were a little bit, I think made him too dim, was kind of what I realized.
就是说……我有点搞不懂他究竟是属于聪明那一挂的还是不聪明那一挂的，我记得我试镜表现还行，但我即兴发挥的部分有点……我觉得把我演得太蠢了，这是我后来意识到的。

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:13.029
But I did, I'm such an Allison Jones fan.
但我真的很喜欢艾莉森·琼斯。

00:33:13.029 --> 00:33:13.869
Yeah.
对。

00:33:13.869 --> 00:33:14.869
And the smartless fans out there.
所有“Smartless”播客的粉丝们。

00:33:14.869 --> 00:33:19.750
Allison Jones is the casting director of The Office and many other great shows.
艾莉森·琼斯是《办公室》以及许多其他优秀剧集的选角导演。

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:27.950
Like I think she is, like any great ensemble comedy of the last 20, 25 years has been Allison Jones, you know?
我觉得她……过去20、25年里任何一部优秀的群戏喜剧背后都有艾莉森·琼斯，你知道吗？

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:33.990
She scoops the most interesting people, the funniest, most watchable people.
她总能挖掘出最有趣的人、最搞笑、最耐看的人。

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:40.549
So I had a good, like she liked me, she brought me in, I remember meeting Greg and I didn't, you know, I didn't get a test deal.
所以我当时表现还行，她挺喜欢我，把我叫去试镜了，我还记得我见到了格雷格，虽然我……我没有拿到试播集的合同。

00:33:40.549 --> 00:33:44.069
I didn't, it was like not, it was not a terrible addition.
我没有……那不是一次糟糕的试镜。

00:33:44.069 --> 00:33:48.230
I just think I didn't quite understand the tone, exactly.
我只是觉得我当时没有完全把握好那个基调，差不多就是这样。

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.230
But I think we all landed in the right place anyway.
不过我想我们最后都找到了适合自己的位置。

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:52.589
How did you hear that?
你是怎么听说这件事的？

00:33:52.589 --> 00:33:54.390
Was that just a huncher to head you heard that?
这只是你的直觉还是你听别人说起过？

00:33:54.390 --> 00:34:02.490
No, Allison saved the sign-in sheets and I saw your name on the sign-in sheet, and Jason Siegel also auditioned for Jim, my believe.
不，艾莉森保存了签到表，我在签到表上看到了你的名字，而且如果我没记错的话，杰森·席格尔也为吉姆试镜过。

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:03.829
Oh my God, oh my God.
我的天呐，我的天呐。

00:34:03.829 --> 00:34:05.849
Was it on the same day that you came in?
是和你来的那天是同一天吗？

00:34:05.849 --> 00:34:08.329
No, it wasn't on the same day that I came in.
不，不是我来的那天。

00:34:08.329 --> 00:34:21.650
It was a different day, but you guys know also that if I hadn't landed Pam on the office, I might have auditioned for Lily because my biggest acting nemesis coming up in the business was Alice and Hannah again.
是不同的一天，但你们也知道，如果我当时没有在《办公室》里拿到帕姆这个角色，我可能会去为莉莉试镜，因为我刚入行时最大的演艺宿敌又是艾莉森·汉尼根。

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:23.050
Oh my God, is that right?
天哪，是这样吗？

00:34:23.050 --> 00:34:24.650
Did you feel like you were stunked?
你当时觉得自己被克制了吗？

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:28.750
She got every part, we would go down to the wire and she would get it.
她拿到了每一个角色，我们总是竞争到最后一刻，然后她就会得到。

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:29.539
Oh my God.
我的天。

00:34:29.539 --> 00:34:31.829
Over and over and over again.
一次又一次，没完没了。

00:34:31.829 --> 00:34:32.869
And any big ones?
有什么大制作吗？

00:34:32.869 --> 00:34:34.550
Any like in Buffy or something?
比如《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》之类的？

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:35.829
No, Buffy was too far too early.
不，巴菲的时间太早了。

00:34:35.829 --> 00:34:37.309
Buffy had already happened.
巴菲已经播过了。

00:34:37.309 --> 00:34:45.630
And so she had a name value that I didn't have, but we were like, you know, very similar in our type.
所以她当时有我没有的名气，但你知道的，我们的戏路非常相似。

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:51.699
And I just remember I would get to a test deal and I'd go in the waiting room.
我只记得每次进到试戏的候场室。

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:54.980
And if she was sitting there, I'd be like, damn it.
如果她坐在那儿，我就会想：该死。

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:56.340
Again?
怎么又是她？

00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:00.380
Every actor has like two or three nemeses.
每个演员都有两三个宿敌。

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:01.500
Like everyone has them.
就像每个人都有一样。

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:04.420
Mine was Rachel Harris, Jamie Dimba.
我的是瑞秋·哈里斯、杰米·丁巴。

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:08.380
We'd walk in and be like, oh, someone wanted the sassy blonde lady.
我们一走进去就会想：哦，有人想要那个说话刻薄的金发女郎了。

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:09.219
Yeah.
对。

00:35:09.219 --> 00:35:10.460
Yes.
没错。

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:12.179
Oh my God, that's so funny.
天哪，太搞笑了。

00:35:12.179 --> 00:35:15.699
And if it wasn't Alison Handigan, it was Mary Lynn Ryskop.
如果不是艾莉森·汉尼根，那就是玛丽·林恩·莱斯卡布。

00:35:15.699 --> 00:35:22.420
So those were like, those were my two big women that I was always like getting down to the wire.
所以，那些就是我那两个总是竞争到最后的劲敌。

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:23.260
This is amazing.
这太神奇了。

00:35:23.260 --> 00:35:25.860
I did not know that you would have been a great little leaf by the way.
顺便说一句，我以前不知道你会是一个很棒的莉莉。

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:26.360
So much.
真的非常适合。

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:26.956
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:35:26.956 --> 00:35:32.382
I mean, I can't imagine, I mean, that's the thing I can't imagine anyone else now, as well.
我的意思是，我简直无法想象，现在我也无法想象别人来演了。

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:44.077
Do you guys know, something I noticed about my early years, because you, I was auditioning so much, you start to notice like, what are the conditions that allow you to be your best and what are the conditions that throw you?
你们知道吗，我注意到自己早年经历中的一件事——因为我试镜实在太多了——你开始注意到，什么样的条件能让你发挥出最好水平，什么样的条件会让你发挥失常？

00:35:44.077 --> 00:35:51.975
When I walked into a waiting room, if I was the only person there or if I was the only man there, I would have an 85% better chance of booking the job.
当我走进候场室时，如果我是那里唯一的人，或者如果我是唯一的男性，我拿下这个工作几率会高出85%。

00:35:51.975 --> 00:35:57.050
The moment I saw another actor roll in, I was like, there were some I mean, almost psychological.
但只要我看到另一个演员晃悠着走进来，我就觉得……这意味着竞争，几乎有点心理战的味道。

00:35:57.050 --> 00:35:59.010
They're seeing other actors for this.
他们还在为这个角色看其他演员。

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:00.769
This is great, you know?
这太棒了，你知道吗？

00:36:00.769 --> 00:36:03.690
Or you see them and you're like, you were supposed to wear a suit?
或者你看到他们，然后心想：你居然穿了西装来？

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:06.489
Like you start like questioning what you wore.
你会开始质疑自己穿的衣服。

00:36:06.489 --> 00:36:07.769
Yes, all of it.
没错，全都是这样。

00:36:07.769 --> 00:36:18.010
It really would, I would even try, when I was signing in, I trained myself not to look at the other names because it would so rattle me if I saw like any of my nemeses on the list.
甚至真的会这样，我签到时甚至训练自己不要去看其他人的名字，因为如果在名单上看到我的任何宿敌，我会心神大乱。

00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:18.849
Yeah.
对。

00:36:18.849 --> 00:36:20.889
It really became like a psychological game.
这真的变成了一场心理战。

00:36:20.889 --> 00:36:23.489
Like I had to create the best conditions for myself.
我必须为自己创造最佳的状态。

00:36:23.489 --> 00:36:33.250
And this old man, he musted me, fell in love with you, New York City.
然后这个老头，他一定迷上你了，纽约市。

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:37.880
And now, commercials.
现在，插播广告。

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:41.679
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，播客《Hyper-Fixed》的主持人。

00:36:41.679 --> 00:36:45.159
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会接受听众的烦恼并试着帮他们解决。

00:36:45.159 --> 00:36:48.320
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的问题：“我30岁了，害怕在纽约开车。”

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:52.119
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者：“为什么我在倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？”

00:36:52.119 --> 00:36:54.639
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:36:54.639 --> 00:37:00.750
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么烦恼，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的背后隐形系统。

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:03.230
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:18.429
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或访问hyperfixedpod.com找到它。

00:37:18.429 --> 00:37:21.659
End of commercials, back to show.
广告结束，回到节目。

00:37:21.659 --> 00:37:28.539
Craig, one of my favorite little beats in the pilot is the throwing of the drink in the face.
克雷格，试播集中我最喜欢的搞笑桥段之一，就是把饮料泼在脸上。

00:37:28.539 --> 00:37:29.380
Yeah.
对。

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:39.019
How you turned that thing that we've seen in multiple romantic comedies, Like you put this, a new spin on it.
你是怎么把这个我们在多部浪漫喜剧中见过的老梗，玩出了新花样。

00:37:39.019 --> 00:37:43.500
It was so fun, it made the character of Robin so fun.
太有趣了，这让罗宾这个角色变得妙趣横生。

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:45.300
It was super charming.
超级有魅力。

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:46.500
I loved that moment.
我超喜欢那个时刻。

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:47.340
I'm glad.
我很庆幸。

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:48.739
That's one of my favorite moments in the pilot too.
这也是我在试播集中最喜欢的时刻之一。

00:37:48.739 --> 00:37:53.780
In part because it connects Ted and Robin so much and people are just, we're just meeting Kobe Smolders, right?
部分原因是因为它将泰德和罗宾紧密地联系在了一起，当时大家才刚刚认识寇碧·史莫德斯，对吧？

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:56.099
Kobe Smolders had not done a lot leading up to this.
寇碧·史莫德斯在这之前并没有拍过很多作品。

00:37:56.099 --> 00:37:59.300
Like she turns around in that green sweater.
就像她穿着那件绿色毛衣转过身来。

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:00.739
Yes, the green sweater.
对，绿色毛衣。

00:38:00.739 --> 00:38:04.139
I was like, what a great character reveal moment.
当时我就想：真是个绝佳的角色出场时刻。

00:38:04.139 --> 00:38:05.900
I'm just like, who is this gal?
我当时就觉得：这姑娘是谁啊？

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:06.739
Yeah.
对。

00:38:06.739 --> 00:38:07.579
It's an old movie moment.
这是一个老电影式的桥段。

00:38:07.579 --> 00:38:08.420
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:10.179
Is it an old movie across the crowded room?
是那种隔着拥挤人群的经典电影桥段吗？

00:38:10.179 --> 00:38:11.019
It really is.
确实是。

00:38:11.019 --> 00:38:13.099
It really worked in the connection between the two of them.
它确实起到了将两人联系起来的作用。

00:38:13.099 --> 00:38:18.820
And again, these guys had only just met, but had bonded a lot in that process we talked about.
而且话说回来，这几个人其实才刚见面，但在我们之前聊过的那个试镜过程中，已经建立起了很深的羁绊。

00:38:18.820 --> 00:38:21.300
You do bond in a hurry going to all these auditions.
跑各种试镜确实能在短时间内建立起革命友情。

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:22.539
It's very high pressure.
那压力太大了。

00:38:22.539 --> 00:38:28.260
And the idea in that moment, Jenna, that the reason I like the moment so much too is they're yes-ending each other.
珍娜，那个时刻最棒的地方在于，我之所以那么喜欢那个桥段，是因为他们在互相顺着对方的话演下去。

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:30.619
Those two characters are doing a weird little improv game.
那两个角色在玩一个奇怪的小即兴游戏。

00:38:30.619 --> 00:38:35.500
They've met three seconds ago, And they're doing it to make her friend feel better who just got dumped.
他们才认识三秒钟。

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:39.539
Her friend who just got dumped and sitting in the corner can't know she's flirting with a guy.
他们这么做是为了让刚被甩的朋友感觉好一点。

00:38:39.539 --> 00:38:41.739
So they act out this little pretend scene.
她那个刚被甩、坐在角落里的朋友绝对不能发现她在跟一个男人调情。

00:38:41.739 --> 00:38:43.619
And that's such a great connection.
所以他们当场演了这么一出假戏。

00:38:43.619 --> 00:38:47.739
And yeah, that kind of launches the whole series in a way from that moment.
这种连接感太棒了。

00:38:47.739 --> 00:39:05.340
You know what occurred to me, it's just occurring to me now, Craig, that like what you and Carter were doing was on some level trying to create a world that was both real and recognizable, while also honoring that you were completely influenced by romantic comedies of the last like 50 to 60 years.
某种程度上，从那一刻起，整个系列剧就此拉开了序幕。

00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:08.099
There were certain tropes that you were like hitting.
你知道我刚才意识到什么吗，克雷格，我直到现在才意识到：你和卡特在某种程度上所做的事情，是试图创造一个既真实又让人有认同感的世界，同时又向过去五六十年来深深影响你们的浪漫喜剧致敬。

00:39:08.099 --> 00:39:15.219
And one thing we talk about on the show is that like, it's a real world where also like magic exists.
你们在片中致敬了某些特定的套路。

00:39:15.219 --> 00:39:16.820
And telepathy exists.
我们在节目中讨论过的一点是，这是一个真实的世界，但魔法也同时存在。

00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:23.900
Like you guys really allowed the universe to be enchanted in how I met your mother while also being incredibly real.
心灵感应也存在。

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:26.659
Which I think is like a real deft balancing act.
在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中，你们确实让这个世界充满了魔幻色彩，同时也保持了极度的真实。

00:39:26.659 --> 00:39:28.460
Yeah, well, I mean, thank you.
我觉得这是一种非常高明的平衡艺术。

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:31.099
I'm glad to be cast you as Ted.
好吧，谢谢夸奖。

00:39:31.099 --> 00:39:31.940
I like you.
我很高兴选中你来演泰德。

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:32.940
You're a good man.
我挺喜欢你的。

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:34.380
Look at you around.
你是个好人。

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:35.460
You're gonna keep you around.
看你表现得不错，我们要把你留下来。

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:37.980
Yeah, we didn't know what we were doing.
其实当时我们也不知道自己在做什么。

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:46.739
We wanted to write the show that broke some rules and we were so lucky to have this tool of the future narrator.
我们只想写一部打破一些常规的剧，而且我们非常幸运地拥有了“未来旁白”这个叙事工具。

00:39:46.739 --> 00:39:49.139
The future narrator, this is his memory, right?
未来的旁白，这都是他的记忆，对吧？

00:39:49.139 --> 00:40:02.179
So sometimes his memory could be photorealistic and sometimes his memory could be absolutely unreliable and even insane and be tinged with magic, tinged with the way that we retroactively put magic and put shape on our lives from the future.
所以有时候他的记忆可以是逼真的，有时他的记忆又完全不可靠、甚至荒诞不经，还带有魔幻色彩，带有我们从未来回溯过去、为生活赋予魔幻和形状的方式。

00:40:02.179 --> 00:40:03.179
Yes, romanticized.
是的，被浪漫化了。

00:40:03.179 --> 00:40:04.260
The romanticized version, right?
也就是被浪漫化的版本，对吧？

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:05.260
There's something there.
这其中确实有些门道。

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:07.179
And we had that tool.
而我们当时拥有这个工具。

00:40:07.179 --> 00:40:29.099
And I thought about this watching the office when we talked about your guys episode, like the tool of just the verite, the cinema verite, the documentary style brings you into such a mood piece in the office, like, and the way that like the talking heads allow you to cut and edit and shape like, so the office I assume is very much written in post as well, right?
当我们在讨论你们的节目时，我看着《办公室》想到了这一点，比如真实电影（cinema vérité）和纪录片风格这种工具，把观众带入《办公室》那种充满氛围感的境地中，还有那些直视镜头的访谈（talking heads）允许你进行剪辑、编辑和塑造——所以我想《办公室》很大程度上也是在后期制作中写出来的，对吧？

00:40:29.099 --> 00:40:35.473
You're in an edit room and there's a lot of rearranging and shaping and stuff and that narrator the narrator enabled us.
你们在剪辑室里，有大量的重新排列、塑造之类的工作，而旁白那个旁白机制赋予了我们这种能力。

00:40:35.473 --> 00:40:43.599
We were rewriting those in the edit room all the time and to create those moments like the across the crowded room moment, that's like a narrator moment.
我们一直在剪辑室里重写那些台词，为了创造那些时刻，比如隔着拥挤房间对望的瞬间，那就像是一个旁白时刻。

00:40:43.599 --> 00:41:00.860
You know, that is shaped by the future narrator that becomes a little movie and these little tricks, the little tricks of the office, the little bag of tricks of how much your mother, those things that set it aside from every other show, those are the reasons these shows still speak to people 20 years later, I think, right?
你知道的，那是被未来的旁白所塑造的，变成了一部小电影，正是这些小把戏，《办公室》的小把戏，《老爸老妈浪漫史》的小把戏，那些让它区别于其他所有节目的东西，我认为这就是这些节目在20年后的今天依然能打动人们的原因，对吧？

00:41:00.860 --> 00:41:01.940
There's what makes it special.
这就是它特别的地方。

00:41:01.940 --> 00:41:16.219
We've noticed as the show's gone on because we're like just kind of getting into the second season, but like how the writers on how I make a mother and I feel this probably is true about the office, you learn how to use the convention to maximum effect.
随着节目的推进——因为我们现在差不多刚进入第二季——我们注意到，就像《老爸老妈浪漫史》的编剧们一样（我觉得《办公室》大概也是如此），你们学会了如何将这种模式的效用发挥到极致。

00:41:16.219 --> 00:41:24.059
Like the talking heads, I'm sure there were probably, I'm guessing that if you had a narrative problem, you could actually solve it with a talking head.
比如直视镜头的访谈，我敢肯定，如果你们遇到了叙事上的难题，实际上可以通过访谈镜头来解决。

00:41:24.059 --> 00:41:28.568
You could connect something with a talking head too, like a storyline.
你也可以用访谈镜头来连接某些东西，比如一条故事线。

00:41:28.568 --> 00:41:30.500
Totally, it's an editing tool.
完全是这样，它就是一个剪辑工具。

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:36.699
There's a thing in episode five, which is where they go to this club and they can't hear each other.
在第五集里有个情节，他们去了一个俱乐部，互相听不见对方说话。

00:41:36.699 --> 00:41:38.300
So it's all subtitled.
所以全都是字幕。

00:41:38.300 --> 00:41:47.420
