比如美丽的手工制品之类的。

00:58:50.559 --> 00:58:54.199
I mean, our community is so warm.
我是说，我们的听众群体真的太温暖了。

00:58:54.199 --> 00:59:01.320
And just has been with us from the beginning and all the way as we've figured out this whole like podcasting thing.
而且从一开始就一直陪伴着我们，伴随着我们摸索出整个播客的门道。

00:59:01.320 --> 00:59:05.079
And I'll just say that you guys set this up during your episode really well.
我只想说，你们在节目中把这个引子铺垫得非常好。

00:59:05.079 --> 00:59:11.079
Just you guys talked about your international fan base, the idea of people using this show to get through hard times.
你们谈到了你们的国际粉丝群体，谈到了人们通过这部剧度过艰难时期的想法。

00:59:11.079 --> 00:59:11.920
We're on topic?
我们在切题吗？

00:59:11.920 --> 00:59:12.960
Yes, you set it up beautifully.
是的，你们铺垫得太美妙了。

00:59:12.960 --> 00:59:13.960
Take it away.
开始吧。

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:16.360
So this is today's letter.
那么，这就是今天的信。

00:59:16.360 --> 00:59:34.920
Just to set this up, for those of you who don't know, the how I met your mother Barr McLarens, the bar in the show is based on a bar in New York City called McGee's that Craig correct me if I'm wrong, you and Carter and the Letterman writers would go to this bar after a hard day's work on the David Letterman show.
为了铺垫一下，对于那些不知道的人来说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》里的麦克拉伦酒吧（Bar McLarens），也就是剧中的酒吧，是以纽约市的一家名为麦基（McGee's）的酒吧为原型的。克雷格，如果我记错了请纠正我，你、卡特还有莱特曼的编剧们在戴夫·莱特曼节目辛苦工作一天后，都会去这家酒吧。

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:36.760
Okay, so here's our letter.
好吧，这是我们的信。

00:59:36.760 --> 00:59:39.159
Hi guys, so excited for this podcast.
嗨，大家好，对这个播客超级期待。

00:59:39.880 --> 00:59:44.679
My name is Javiera, and I must start by saying that I'm from Chile.
我叫哈维拉（Javiera），我必须先说我是来自智利的。

00:59:44.679 --> 00:59:49.400
Ted would know since he loves Naruto poems, so I'm sorry for any grammar mistakes.
泰德肯定知道，因为他很喜欢纳罗托（Naruto）的诗，所以如果有语法错误请见谅。

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:54.519
You asked about how himium changed lives, and I would like to share my story with you.
你们问过《老爸老妈浪漫史》是如何改变生活的，我想和大家分享我的故事。

00:59:54.519 --> 00:59:58.599
I started watching the show around third season when I was in bed rest for a back injury.
我大约在第三季时开始看这部剧，当时我因为背部受伤卧床休息。

00:59:58.599 --> 01:00:02.039
My sister rushed to see what happened to me because she thought she heard me crying.
我姐姐急忙跑来看我出了什么事，因为她以为听到我在哭。

01:00:02.599 --> 01:00:04.599
Turned out, I was laughing my ass off.
结果呢，我笑得前仰后合。

01:00:04.599 --> 01:00:17.119
In 2016, I went to London to do a PhD, but two years later, I had a serious life-threatening health problem that led me to an emergency surgery and pretty bad PTSD afterwards.
2016年，我去伦敦攻读博士学位，但两年后，我遭遇了严重的、危及生命的健康问题，导致我接受了紧急手术，之后患上了相当严重的创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）。

01:00:17.119 --> 01:00:22.099
I stayed for a year in London before eventually having to quit the PhD program to work on my recovery.
我在伦敦待了一年，最终不得不放弃博士学位，专心休养。

01:00:22.099 --> 01:00:25.199
That year was hard, beyond anything I could describe.
那一年之艰难，无法用言语形容。

01:00:25.199 --> 01:00:31.480
Besides the pain, every night I had panic attacks because of the flashbacks and because I was afraid to go to sleep.
除了身体的痛苦，每天晚上因为闪回以及害怕入睡，我都会经历恐慌发作。

01:00:31.480 --> 01:00:41.280
I felt really vulnerable because I was completely alone, an ocean and a whole different time zone between me and anyone who could take care of me or bring me calm.
我感到非常脆弱，因为我完全孤身一人，我和任何能照顾我或给我带来平静的人之间隔着一个大洋和一个完全不同的时区。

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:46.199
So to be able to sleep, I started playing how I met your mother on my computer through the night.
所以为了能够入睡，我开始整夜在电脑上播放《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

01:00:46.199 --> 01:00:52.760
I knew the voices, the stories, it felt like having the company of people I knew and it helped me to control my crisis.
我熟悉这些声音、这些故事，感觉就像有熟悉的人陪伴着我，这帮我控制住了情绪崩溃。

01:00:52.760 --> 01:00:55.920
The show helped me to survive and to cope.
这部剧帮我活了下来，帮我度过了难关。

01:00:55.920 --> 01:00:56.980
Still does.
现在依然如此。

01:00:56.980 --> 01:01:05.980
Anyway, a year after I returned to Chile, I met a guy who asked me about my favorite TV show, You Can Guess, and he said he liked it a lot too.
总之，在我回到智利一年后，我遇到一个男生，他问我最喜欢什么电视剧，你们猜得到，他说他也非常喜欢。

01:01:05.980 --> 01:01:07.980
Although I'm pretty sure it was also a strategy.
虽然我很确信这也是一种追求策略。

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:12.980
Four years later in New York City, he asked me to marry him at McGee's.
四年后在纽约市，他在麦基酒吧向我求婚了。

01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:15.980
And today, he is my husband.
而今天，他成了我的丈夫。

01:01:15.980 --> 01:01:16.980
Oh my gosh.
哦天哪。

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:18.980
You ask about how the show changed my life?
你们问这部剧是如何改变我的生活的？

01:01:18.980 --> 01:01:23.980
That doesn't even come near to describe what it has done and what it means to me.
这甚至都无法形容它所做的一切以及它对我的意义。

01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:24.480
to me.
对我而言。

01:01:24.480 --> 01:01:25.068
Oh my God.
哦天哪。

01:01:25.068 --> 01:01:32.483
That is one hell of an ad for McGee's, which is still open and serving delicious beer and food at 55th between Broadway and...
这绝对是为麦基酒吧做的一则绝佳广告，它现在依然营业，在55街和百老汇之间为你提供美味的啤酒和食物……

01:01:32.483 --> 01:01:34.308
Greg's a part owner, obviously.
格雷格显然是部分所有者。

01:01:34.308 --> 01:01:35.956
Yeah, I've invested heavily.
对，我投了很多钱。

01:01:35.956 --> 01:01:39.134
I've most of my how much money's been tied up in that.
我的大部分钱都砸进去了。

01:01:39.134 --> 01:01:40.546
Well, thank you, Javier.
好吧，谢谢你，哈维拉。

01:01:40.546 --> 01:01:41.841
That was so beautiful.
这太美妙了。

01:01:41.841 --> 01:01:54.906
And, you know, almost like what Jenna was talking about earlier, although I think it was on when we were guests on your podcast, but just This is the only moment I'm gonna brush shoulders with this one person on the earth.
而且，你知道的，有点像珍娜之前谈到的，虽然我想那是在我们作为嘉宾上你们播客的时候，但这只是……这是我一生中唯一一次与这个人擦肩而过的时刻。

01:01:54.760 --> 01:01:55.599
Yeah.
是啊。

01:01:55.599 --> 01:02:12.320
We don't know, like, if you've seen and fallen in love with how I met your mother, and like, it's gonna change your life somehow, whether it changes your interior or it changes how you connect with your future husband, like, we're affecting each other all the time, and I find that very moving.
我们不知道，比如说，如果你看过并爱上了《老爸老妈浪漫史》，然后……它会以某种方式改变你的生活，无论是改变你的内心世界，还是改变你与未来丈夫的联系，我们一直在相互影响，我觉得这非常感人。

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:19.960
Yeah, and whether you're like on a TV show or not on a TV show or just going about your life, you never know your impact on someone else.
是的，无论你是在拍电视剧，还是没拍电视剧，亦或是只是过着自己的生活，你永远不知道自己对别人有什么影响。

01:02:19.960 --> 01:02:20.460
Yeah.
是啊。

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:20.960
Yeah.
是啊。

01:02:20.960 --> 01:02:33.744
Could we have imagined like when you're when you guys were kids watching like Diane Chambers on Cheers or you were visiting America and just Angela soaking up a little bit of American TV before you went back to to where was it in Jakarta?
我们能想象吗，就像当你们还是孩子的时候看《干杯酒吧》（Cheers）里的黛安·钱伯斯（Diane Chambers），或者你访问美国，安吉拉在回到……那是哪里来着？雅加达？之前，稍微吸收了一点美国电视节目。

01:02:33.744 --> 01:02:36.322
Yeah to Jakarta and little house on the prairie.
对，去雅加达，还有《草原小屋》（Little House on the Prairie）。

01:02:36.322 --> 01:02:52.382
Like could you guys imagine like what would you say to yourselves like now like how do you convey that idea to past you that like someday the show will mean that to to people it's the strangers around the world it's amazing because because I know the office means that to so many millions of people.
就像你们能想象吗……你们现在会对当时的自己说什么？——你们怎么把那个想法传达给过去的自己，即总有一天这部剧对人们、对世界各地的陌生人来说会有这样的意义，这太神奇了，因为我知道《办公室》对千百万人来说就是这样的存在。

01:02:51.920 --> 01:02:55.980
Well, you know, it's what I have in my head whenever I'm meeting fans.
嗯，你知道的，每当我见到粉丝时，我脑子里想的就是这个。

01:02:57.639 --> 01:03:05.820
I imagine what it would have been like for me to meet Shelley Long.
我想象如果是我去见雪莉·朗（Shelley Long）会是什么样。

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:07.420
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

01:03:07.420 --> 01:03:09.940
And what that would have meant to me.
那对意味着什么。

01:03:09.940 --> 01:03:14.260
And I realize that I'm on the other side of that now.
而我意识到我现在正处于那个角色的另一端。

01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:18.730
And so I wanna give former Jenna.
所以我想要给过去的珍娜。

01:03:18.730 --> 01:03:19.230
Yeah.
对。

01:03:19.230 --> 01:03:27.848
Exactly what she would have wanted in that moment because my love of Cheers and what it meant to me was very, very, very real.
提供她在那一刻最想要的东西，因为我对《干杯酒吧》的热爱以及它对我的意义是非常、非常、非常真实的。

01:03:28.090 --> 01:03:28.690
Yeah.
是啊。

01:03:29.690 --> 01:03:31.650
And so it's exciting for me.
所以这让我很激动。

01:03:31.650 --> 01:03:40.329
It's kind of when you talk about parenting, like in your life, if you become a parent, now you get to have the other side of the parent-child relationship.
这有点像你谈到为人父母时，在你的生活中，如果你成了父母，现在你就能体验到父母与孩子关系的另一面了。

01:03:40.329 --> 01:03:43.250
You had the child one and now you get to have the parent one.
你曾是孩子，现在你成了父母。

01:03:43.530 --> 01:03:49.942
And so I get to kind of move places in the sort of like, I guess, fan.
所以我可以转换一下位置，大概在……粉丝的立场上。

01:03:49.942 --> 01:03:53.607
I don't know what the other position is.
我不知道另一个立场是什么。

01:03:53.607 --> 01:03:54.107
Yeah.
是啊。

01:03:54.107 --> 01:03:57.771
You know, creator, artist, relationship.
你知道的，创作者、艺术家与粉丝的关系。

01:03:57.730 --> 01:03:58.606
And so, yeah.
所以，是的。

01:03:58.606 --> 01:04:00.022
Super famous TV star.
超级著名的电视明星。

01:04:00.022 --> 01:04:02.853
Just call yourself a super famous TV star.
直接称自己为超级著名的电视明星吧。

01:04:02.853 --> 01:04:03.729
I don't know.
我不知道。

01:04:03.730 --> 01:04:06.278
Oh, we're always so uncomfortable with that.
哦，我们对那个总是很不自在。

01:04:06.278 --> 01:04:14.272
That's a beautiful analogy, that parenthood one, because it's also just like speaks to how nurturing you are in your relationship to fans.
那个为人父母的比喻太美妙了，因为它也恰恰说明了你在与粉丝的关系中是多么地具有滋养性。

01:04:14.272 --> 01:04:22.729
Like, these letters we got, like, that's an amazingly beautiful story that we would never have known known that story prior to doing this podcast.
就像，我们收到的这些信件，这是一个极其美妙的故事，如果不是做这个播客，我们永远不会知道这个故事。

01:04:22.730 --> 01:04:35.090
And my wife has said the letters you're reading on the air and the people who call in and write in, that's .001% of the stories.
我妻子曾说过，你们在节目中读到的信件，以及那些打电话和写信的人，只占所有故事的0.001%。

01:04:35.090 --> 01:04:40.489
Like there's so many stories that we'll never know that we're not going to hear, but are very real.
就像有那么多我们永远不会知道、也听不到的故事，但它们却真实存在。

01:04:40.489 --> 01:04:41.489
Yeah.
是的。

01:04:41.489 --> 01:04:42.489
Yeah.
是的。

01:04:42.489 --> 01:04:48.318
I feel that way, too, when we read someone's letter and they'll start off with, I have no idea if you'll ever see this.
每当读到某人的信，他们以“我根本不知道你们是否会看到这个”开头时，我也有同感。

01:04:48.318 --> 01:04:48.818
Yeah.
是的。

01:04:48.818 --> 01:04:54.941
And I think about them sitting somewhere writing that and pouring their heart out, not even knowing if we're going to see it.
我就会想到他们坐在某个地方写下那些信，倾注了所有的心血，甚至不知道我们到底能不能看到。

01:04:54.941 --> 01:04:56.705
And I'm just so incredibly grateful.
而我真的感到非常感激。

01:04:56.449 --> 01:04:58.238
I sit in a lot of gratitude.
我心中充满了感激之情。

01:04:58.238 --> 01:04:58.738
Yeah.
是的。

01:04:58.738 --> 01:05:03.403
And we really, we really read a lot of them that we then never even read.
而且我们真的、真的读了很多信，其中许多我们甚至都没在节目里念过。

01:05:03.403 --> 01:05:04.617
So we take that in.
所以我们都把这些收下了。

01:05:04.617 --> 01:05:20.270
Angela and I will go into the podcasting office and we'll spread out that that we get physical mail, and we get digital mail, and we'll sit and we'll trade letters, and we'll say, oh, look at this person, they just graduated from nursing school.
我和安吉拉会去播客办公室，把收到的实体信件和电子信件铺开，我们坐在那里交换信件，然后说：“噢，你看这个人，他刚从护校毕业。”

01:05:20.090 --> 01:05:25.489
Like, we get the stuff, and we- We're invited to someone's wedding, isn't, look at their invitation, this was amazing.
我们收到各种各样的东西，我们——有人邀请我们去参加婚礼，看他们的请柬，这太棒了。

01:05:25.489 --> 01:05:27.449
Oh, I've been invited to so many weddings.
噢，我收到了好多婚礼邀请呢。

